1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I have old/new information for Charlotte. Her name was not, as you say, Bounos or Bouniot. It was Bernon. First, it should be noted that she was much younger than Jean Papineau, as she married Samuel Seely in or ne
2 CONC ar Stamford, CT in 1709 and subsequently had eight children by him (per letter of Mar. 16, 1941 to Carl Poppino from Frank Bradford Simpson, Ridgewood, NY). Jean's partner in the chamoiserie was Gabriel Bernon. Gabriel was born in
2 CONC  1644 and fathered ten children before he reached Oxford (per Genealogical Guide To The Early Settlers of America. New York; Spirit of '76 Press, 1898).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unlikely that Jean (as Bernon's partner) was a mere youth at that time, especially since he died between the birth of his second son, Pierre, in 1706, and his widow's remarriage in 1709. Bernon, a Huguenot, was a man of affa
2 CONC irs, who "settled in Boston where he resided some years" (per Genealogical Guide aforementioned). He also spent many years in Newport and Providence, R.I. He had five more children after reaching America. It is thought that Charlo
2 CONC tte was one of the children he brought to the New World.
2 CONT
2 CONT The records of those days were kept in script, of course. An "o" could look like an "e", and an "s" could look like an "n". The record of Charlotte was from the baptismal record of her second son Pierre, baptised at the Huguenot c
2 CONC hurch on Manhattan Island on August 14, 1706 (Reverend J. Laborie, minister, formerly the minister at New Oxford). One further point is that, in the sparse settlements of those days, it is extremely unlikely that there would be tw
2 CONC o French families bearing names so similar as Bernon and Bounos. Also, Bernon is a common French surname, whereas Bounos is rare, and is questioned whether it exists at all.  I obtained this through the 1969 revision of the Poppin
2 CONC o history given to my father in about 1972.
2 CONT
2 CONT If anyone can give me information.resources other than the Popenoe web page, that would be great. I am not seeing anything concrete tat corroborates either account is true
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted by Mary J. King (Poppino)  This would be my 7th great grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte Bouniot:  Rhode Island/New York
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte had two children by Papineau:  John (Jean--date of birth unknown; perhaps 1704-5); and Peter (Pierre), baptized in 1706. Her maiden name was written as Bounos when Pierre was christened. [1]   In her second marriage, to 
2 CONC Samuel Seely in Stamford, Connecticut, May 13, 1709, she is listed as Charlotte Popino.[2]   Between 1710 and 1724 she had eight more children by Seely.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Bounos exists in Greece but has not been found in North America during the colonial period, leading me to conclude that the actual name was different.  So I began a search for her possible real name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Frenchtown, the French settlement at Narragansett (East Greenwich, RI) was earlier and larger than New Oxford, involving forty or fifty families. Unfortunately, they bought land with a bad title and, in 1691, were forced to abando
2 CONC n it by the court and the adjacent English settlers whose claim was upheld.  Twenty-one of the families went to New York, seven went to Boston, and René Grignon went to New Oxford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the leaders in Frenchtown was Pierre Bouniot--variously spelled Bonniot, Bonyot, and Boigniot.[4]   Bouniot with a silent "t" sounds a lot like Bounos. Bouniot was an educated and pious man who served as elder and secretary
2 CONC  of the church, and frequently read the scripture to the congregation.  In 1688 he asked permission to set up a private bench for his family in the church--the first to do so--for which he paid 3 shillings a year.  This suggests t
2 CONC hat he had a family consisting of more than infants, who would have need for a bench.  In 1689, his wife Elizabeth de Faucquembergue gave birth to a son Ezechiel.  Two other children, Marthe and Jean, were also born to them at Nar
2 CONC ragansett, but both died. When Jean was born in 1690, Abraham and Charlotte Faucquembergue presented him for baptism.  Since these names do not appear as landholders, they were presumably part of Bouniot's family, probably his wif
2 CONC e’s parents. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Charlotte is not mentioned.  We might assume that she was named after her grandmother and was born before 1686 when the Narragansett records begin (but not too much before since Samuel Seely was born in 1687 and would be unlik
2 CONC ely to marry a much older woman.)  After the community broke up, we next find the name Bonyot and Boigniot in the records of the French Church in New York (where Pierre Papineau was baptized), showing various children of Ezechiel 
2 CONC and his wife Ester being baptized between 1716 and 1721.  How might Charlotte Bouniot have met Jean Papineau?  Both families appear to have had a connection with Niort in France and may have belonged to the same church there.  The
2 CONC  New Oxford and Narragansett communities were connected with each other and with Boston.  The three American churches maintained a close communion.  The route from Frenchtown passed through Providence and then split to New Oxford 
2 CONC and to Boston.  During one period, the minister was in Boston but returned to Frenchtown once every two months for services.   Remember that René Grignon, Papineau's partner in New Oxford, had lived in Frenchtown before moving to 
2 CONC New Oxford. where Bernon set them both up in business (perhaps providing a return on Papineau’s father’s £460). Jean Papineau could have met Charlotte Bouniot in New Oxford or Boston or New York and could have married her around 1
2 CONC 703 in New Oxford when he was 25 and she would be 18 or slightly older.  Jean's move to New York in 1704 would be not only a matter of following Jacques Laborie, his minister, but also of rejoining Charlotte's family. The other po
2 CONC ssibility is that Jean married Charlotte in New York after leaving New Oxford in 1704, and that Jean Jr. was born in 1705, and Pierre in 1706.  In those days people usually had a first child shortly after marriage and Charlotte wa
2 CONC s obviously a highly fertile woman.  Note that her first child bore the name of her husband; her second child that of her presumed father.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1709, Charlotte married Samuel Seely in Stamford, Connecticut, about 40 miles to the east.
2 CONT
2 CONT Next
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [1]  Alfred V Wittmayer, Registers of the French Church of New York, reprint GPC, 1968. I have looked at a photocopy of the original register at the New York Historical Society and it clearly says Bounos to me. Bear in mind, howev
2 CONC er, that in those days there were no established spellings and a scribe would merely write down what the name sounded like to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] Rev. R. E. Huntington, Stamford Registration of Births, Marriages and Deaths, Stamford, CT 1874, photocopy 1995 NEHGS
2 CONT
2 CONT [3] Descendants of Robert Seeley, Vol 1, Seeley Genealogical Society, 1977, p 19.
2 CONT
2 CONT [4] See Records of the French Church at Naragansett, 1686-1691, in NY Genealogical and Biographical Record, Vol 70, 1939, pp 236-241 and Vol 71, pp 51-61. See also Elisha R Potter, Memoir concerning the French Settlers in the Colo
2 CONC ny of Rhode Island, 1879, reprinted by GPC 1968. In Thierry Du Pasquier, Généalogies Huguenotes, Paris, Editions Christian, 1985 (in the Huguenot Society Library, NYC) the Bonniots are discussed as an important Huguenot family in 
2 CONC France (judges, lawyers, merchants and big landholders) with a long connection to Niort—one was mayor in 1373—but the Rhode Island family is listed as unlinked (p 240). I have no documentary proof that Charlotte Bounos was Charlot
2 CONC te Bouniot, but the evidence gives this a high probability.
2 CONT
