2 CONT Obadiah was an early resident of Stamford, CT, as several entries in the records show. On Feb. 20, 1648 it was recorded that John Holly had 9 acres of land in Rock Neck, Stamford, bounded by lands of Obadiah Seeley on the north. O
2 CONC badiah and Mary were married in Stamford, CT. and their first child was born there 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1649, Obadiah was listed as a citizen of Stamford. In 1651, Obadiah acknowledged payment of a debt due him from John Lareson. Obadiah died intestate Aug. 25,1657. Inventory of his estate was dated Dec. 24 1665 and mentions his 
2 CONC widow Mary and their sons Obadiah, Cornelius and Jonas. Distribution of estate was dated Sept. 3, 1666. Habakuk Seeley died at the age of 3 in Stamford June, 1658.
2 CONT
2 CONT SGS VOL 1 page, 6
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah Seeley probably came to America with Robert Seeley on the "Arabella" in 1630 (Winthrop Fleet). The "Arabella" left England in March and reached Salem, Mass., June 12, 1630. Notables on board include John Winthrop, the firs
2 CONC t Governor of Massachusettes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records indicate that Obadiah was in Stamford by 1649. He lived in a house he built on the east side of South Street, which is now Atlantic Street. Stamford, in New Haven Colony, was founded in 1641 by secessionists from the Wethe
2 CONC rsfield church. Emigrants from Watertown, Massachusetts, founded Wethersfield, the first English settlement in Connecticut, in the winter of 1634-35--Encyclopædia Britannica.)
2 CONT
