1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Epenetus Holmes: Death and Probate
2 CONT Death Date: Epenetus Holmes died in October 1798 in Greenwich, Fairfield County, CT.
2 CONT The Will: His will was proven in the Stamford Probate District (which handled Greenwich cases) shortly after his death.
2 CONT Status of Sarah: Based on the probate documents, Sarah (Mead) Holmes is not mentioned, which strongly indicates that she predeceased him. In colonial Connecticut, if a wife were still living, the law required she be granted her "d
2 CONC ower thirds" (one-third of the real estate and personal property) unless otherwise provided for.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clues to Her Death Date
2 CONT While a specific day/month for Sarah remains elusive, we can narrow down the window of her death using the following milestones:
2 CONT Lower Bound (1764): Sarah was definitely living at least into the early 1760s, as she was still bearing children (her son Epenetus Jr. was born around this time).
2 CONT Upper Bound (1798): She had passed away by the time Epenetus wrote or finalized the distribution of his estate in 1798.
2 CONT Probable Window: Most local historians estimate her death occurred between 1770 and 1790 in Greenwich.
2 CONT Burial Location
2 CONT Because she died before 1800, she was likely buried in an unmarked grave or a fieldstone-marked grave in one of the following historic Greenwich sites:
2 CONT The Old Burying Ground at Greenwich Point (Elizabeth's Neck): Where many early Holmes family members were laid to rest.
2 CONT Tomac Burying Ground: One of the oldest cemeteries in the area (Old Greenwich).
2 CONT Second Congregational Church Cemetery: Many Meads and Holmeses were members here.
