1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Richard1 Slawson1,2 was born 1585 in Southwark, Surrey, England2. He married Anne Angell3,4 13 May 1610 in St. Savior's, Southwark, Surrey, England4. She was born Abt. 1589 in Southwark, Surrey, England4. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Richard Slawson: According the the SLASON--SLAUSON--SLAWSON--SLOSSON FAMILY: There are undoubtedly other children in this family, but only Thomas and George came to America, the others remaining in England. Thomas SLAWSO
2 CONC N's line dies out after 2 more generations. As the name of SLAWSON does not appear upon records of any of the other settlements up to 1650, it is obvious that George SLAWSON was the pioneer ancestor of the Slason, Slauson, Slawson
2 CONC  and Slosson families in America.[Slosson 11-20-00 Greene.FTW] 
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SLASON, SLAUSON, SLAWSON, SLOSSON FAMILY  Richard Slawson's name on the records was spelled Slawson, but there is nothing  to prove that he wrote it so, or even knew how to write. There is, however,  ample evidence to prove th
2 CONC at this spelling continued to exist in England, and  later migration to America may have been made by individuals using this  spelling. However, from the spelling used on tombstones, it would seem that the  Slawson spelling was an
2 CONC  Anglisized version of Slosson, which, according to  information obtained by the compiler in the United States was known as early as  the twelvth century. There seems to be little doubt that this spelling was in  use considerably 
2 CONC earlier than 1600, and no evidence to prove it had not been  completely abandoned in favor of Slawson by that date. In 1739 this spelling  was again put into use, this time in America - and it is in active use here  today, but is 
2 CONC apparently unknown in England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Richard Slawson and Anne Angell are: 
2 CONT # George2 Slawson, born Bef. 1617 in Southwark, Surrey, England; died 17 Feb 1695 in Stamford, Fairfield Co., Connecticut.
2 CONT # Thomas Slawson, born Abt. 1620 in Southwark, Surrey, England; died Bef. 1655. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * [http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/d/o/n/Darlene-Donovan/GENE1-0001.html Descendants of Richard Slawson]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT According the the SLASON--SLAUSON--SLAWSON--SLOSSON FAMILY: There are undoubtedly other children in this family, but only Thomas and George came to America, the others remaining in England. Thomas SLAWSON's line dies out after 2 m
2 CONC ore generations. As the name of SLAWSON does not appear upon records of any of the other settlements up to 1650, it is obvious that George SLAWSON was the pioneer ancestor of the Slason, Slauson, Slawson and Slosson families in Am
2 CONC erica.[Slosson 11-20-00 Greene.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SLASON, SLAUSON, SLAWSON, SLOSSON FAMILY Richard Slawson's name on the records was spelled Slawson, but there is nothing to prove that he wrote it so, or even knew how to write. There is, however, ample evidence to prove that 
2 CONC this spelling continued to exist in England, and later migration to America may have been made by individuals using this spelling. However, from the spelling used on tombstones, it would seem that the Slawson spelling was an Angli
2 CONC sized version of Slosson, which, according to information obtained by the compiler in the United States was known as early as the twelvth century. There seems to be little doubt that this spelling was in use considerably earlier t
2 CONC han 1600, and no evidence to prove it had not been completely abandoned in favor of Slawson by that date. In 1739 this spelling was again put into use, this time in America - and it is in active use here today, but is apparently u
2 CONC nknown in England
