2 CONT =History of Hazard Wilcox, Jr and Sarah Seely Wilcox=
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Treaty of Ghent ended the war of 1812 on Christmas Eve 1814. * {Sarah was bom in
2 CONT Schaghticoke, Albany (Now Rensselaer), New York}. Her feet first plodded westward through the Mohawk Valley in 1801, having married Hazard Wilcox, Jr., in Albany. In the Mohawk Valley in 1801 there were still some echoes out of th
2 CONC e revolution and even a few out of the earUer French and Indian frecas of her father's day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lying beside Sarah in far-off Utah is her oldest daughter and firstborn, Mary Wilcox Lowry. Mary was bom in "Ossewagotcha" (says the inscription on her stone ) County in "Upper Canada" in 1802. Obviously Sarah and Hazard had emigr
2 CONC ated to Canada through the Mohawk Valley to the Niagara River and into what is now South-eastern Ontario. Albany is soundly located geographically at the confluence of the Mohawk and Hudson Valleys, and in 1801 its 3,000 permanent
2 CONC  residents probably still saw occasional characters in "leatherstockings." Up the Hudson to Lake Camplain and the Richelieu River lay French Canada and the Algonquins. Up the Mohawk lay the Iroquois Confederation and English Canad
2 CONC a. Must there be any question that 1801 Sarah had the Mohawk, the Seneca, the Onandaga and the Hurons on her mind? One may well wonder whether even the name of Tecumseh was not known to her. In the case of Sarah and Hazard, they w
2 CONC ere looking forward to a grant of land in Canada, given to them by the British for services rendered to the crown by Hazard's father, Hazard Wilcox, Sr. The senior Wilcox was a captain in British-American loyalist militia, and he 
2 CONC was killed in the battle at White Plains, New York in 1776, while in pursuit of the rebel general, Washington and McDougal. Hazard, Sr., in other words was a "Tory," and not only a "redcoat," but a captain of "Lobsterbacks."
2 CONT
2 CONT How much that may have meant to Hazard, Jr., no one can now know. One can guess not much. He was an infant when his father died in battle for the Crown and he never knew him. Hazard Jr., was a farmer, and like many farmers in the 
2 CONC 13 states, he wanted good, new land to start his manied life on, a desire to be realized in Upper Canada, thanks to Great Britain. Sarah bore five children in Upper Canada, her first-born being Mary, and all five were born in Emes
2 CONC ttown, Ossewegatchie {Lenox-Addington} County. The Wilcoxes remain frontier Canadians for 10 years, but on the brink of the War of 1812 (1811), Hazard Wilcox made a decision. He removed his family back into American territory and 
2 CONC emigrated farther *{ Southwest into what was then Gallatin County Ilinois. Later to be divided into other counties which included White County, with Carmi Township part of it and Williamson County, which Marion township became a p
2 CONC art. They are listed in the 1820 census in Carmi, White County, Illinois (White County was created in 1815). Six children were born in this area, a set of twins that died and another set of twins, of which one died. Shortly, there
2 CONC after, the Wilcoxes are found in Benton, Saline County (Pulaski County then), Arkansas. Here in 1824, another son John Henry Owen Wilcox was born. (There is also a Benton, Saline, Illinois by Carmi, White, Illinois?) This same yea
2 CONC r of 1824, Sarah saw her first born, Mary, married to a Kentuckian, John Lowry Sr.
2 CONT * {The family then moved in 1826 to Congressional district 60
2 CONT
2 CONT =TIMELINE OF HAZARD WILLCOX=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 25 DEC 1775 HAZARD WILLCOX, JR.' S BIRTH AT WALLUMSCOCK, ALBANY, NEW YORK.
2 CONT * 1777 HAZARD WILLCOX, SR. FLED WITH FAMILY TO CANADA AFTER LOSING HIS PROPERTY IN THE BATTLE OF BENNINGTON.
2 CONT * 02 MAR 1779 HAZARD WILLCOX, SR.'S DEATH. (HAZARD WILCOX, JR. 3 YEARS OLD).
2 CONT * 1779 EUNICE . (HAZARD WILCOX, JR. 3 YEARS OLD).
2 CONT * 14 OCT 1801 HAZARD WILLCOX, JR.'S MARRIAGE TO SARAH SEELEY IN OSWEGATCHIE, ONTARIO, CANADA.
2 CONT * 27 DEC 1810 SABRA WILCOX PURDY'S PETITION LISTED HAZARD WILCOX, JR. AS "OF LOUGHBOROUGH".
2 CONT * 14 MAY 1793 HAZARD JR. AND WILLIAM WILLCOX BOTH PETITION FOR 800 ACRES IN GRANVILLE, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 16 OCT 1802 FIRST CHILD MARY WILLCOX BORN IN ERNESTOWN, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 10 AUG 1804 SECOND CHILD SARAH SEELEY WILLCOX BORN IN ERNESTOWN, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 07 JAN 1806 THIRD CHILD WILLIAM AUGUSTUS WILLCOX BORN IN ERNESTOWN, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 08 MAR 1808 HAZARD JR. WROTE TO THE BOARD LISTED AS "U. E. LOYALIST OF LOUGHBOROUGH".
2 CONT * 19 MAY 1808 FORTH CHILD EUNICE SEELEY WILLCOX BORN IN ERNESTOWN, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 16 JUN 1810 FIFTH CHILD SABRAH WILLCOX BORN IN ERNESTOWN, UPPER CANADA.
2 CONT * 30 JUN 1811 SARAH SEELEY WILLCOX LISTED AS "WIFE OF HAZARD WILLCOX OF LOUGHBOROUGH" IN HER PETITION. 
2 CONT * 26 JAN 1813 SIXTH CHILD JONATHAN HAZARD WILLCOX BORN IN MARION COUNTY, MISSOURI. 
2 CONT * 01 DEC 1815 SEVENTH AND EIGHTH CHILDREN JAMES M. AND JOSEPH S. WILLCOX BIRTHS IN MARION COUNTY, MISSOURI
2 CONT * 16 OCT 1816 HAZARD JR. GRANTED 200 ACRES IN CARTWRIGHT TOWNSHIP, DURHAM COUNTY, CON. 9, LOT 24.
2 CONT * 20 APR 1817 NINTH CHILD STEVEN ENOS WILLCOX BORN IN MARION COUNTY, MISSOURI.
2 CONT * 01 OCT 1821 TENTH AND ELEVENTH CHILDREN CLARISSA JANE AND LEMUEL NIXON WILLCOX BIRTHS IN CARMI, WHITE, ILLINOIS.
2 CONT * 14 JAN 1824 TWELFTH CHILD JOHN HENRY OWEN WILLCOX BORN IN BENTON, SALINE, ARKANSAS.
2 CONT * ABT 1828 THIRTEENTH AND FOURTEENTH CHILD JAMES AND HAZARD WILLCOX BIRTHS.
2 CONT * 16 FEB 1831 HAZARD WILLCOX, JR.'S DIED IN MARION, WILLIAMSON, MISSOURI.
2 CONT
