2 CONC  Canada)''']
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000014138950497?album_type=photos_of_me&end=&photo_id=6000000223413394961&project_id=&start=&tagged_profiles= '''Death Record: November 11, 1859 (57) in Manti, Sanpete County, Utah Territory,
2 CONC  United States''']
2 CONT * Place of Burial: Manti Cemetery, Manti, Sanpete County, Utah, United States
2 CONT * [https://billiongraves.com/grave/Mary-Wilcox-Lowry/16044780?referrer=myhertage '''Mary Wilcox Lowry in Billion Graves'''] 
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/43340918/mary-lowry '''Find A Grave Memorial''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000014138950497?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000082930668029 '''Mary Wilcox'''] on 1 Feb 1824, in Shelby, Tennessee United States
2 CONT
2 CONT =CHILDREN BORN TO MARY:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''James Hazard''', 3 June 1825
2 CONT * '''Hyrum Madison''', 15 March 1827
2 CONT * '''John Jr.,''' 31 January 1829
2 CONT * '''Abner''', 12 October 1831
2 CONT * '''Susan Lucreta,''' 14 March 1834
2 CONT * '''Mary Artimesia,''' 14 March 1834
2 CONT * '''George Moroni,''' 9 August 1836
2 CONT * '''Sarah Jane,'''26 January 1838
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Eunice,''' 16 May 1841
2 CONT * '''William Mahonri,''' 28 April 1844
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: http://lds.org/churchhistory/library/pioneerdetails/1,15791,4018-1-1071,00.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://history.churchofjesuschrist.org/overlandtravel/pioneers/1071/mary-lowry Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847–1868]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lowry, Mary Wilcox'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth Date: 	6 Oct. 1802
2 CONT Death Date: 	'''11 Nov. 1859'''
2 CONT Gender: 	Female Age: 	44
2 CONT
2 CONT Company: 	Edward Hunter - Jacob Foutz Company (1847)
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from "Pioneer Women and Faith and Fortitude Contributed By: Larry G Jensen · 29 April 2014 ·
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was born in Oswegatchie, Ontario, Canada October 16 (?) 1802, a daughter of Hazard Wilcox and Sarah Seeley.  She married John Lowry, 1 february 1824, Madison County Missouri.  Since John had not had the opportunity to attend 
2 CONC school Mary taught him how to read and write.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was a woman of superior intelligence, possessing rare courage and great presence of mind.  She was staunch supporter of her husband in all the various phases of their pioneer life.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's previous wife had died and Mary raised their two children, Sarah and William, as if they were her own.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1825 through 1844 Mary and John became the parents of ten children of their own.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the birth of their first child they traveled to Lewis County Missouri.  Later they moved to Clay County where John bought 80 acres of land.  Persecution of the saints caused them to move to Caldwell County.  In 1843 John was
2 CONC  called to serve a mission which left Mary with many problems to solve.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was asked to assist in helping the poor, the widowed and the orphans leave the persecution of the mobs.  He was seldom home.  His own family was left for the last trip he would make.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the enemies of the church came to the Lowry home and threatened Mary and the children.  They said they would burn the house down over her head if she did not leave by ten o'clock the next morning.  She said she would love 
2 CONC to leave but couldn't until her husband returned with the team and wagon.  The threat was repeated.  Mary reached behind the door, picked up the old flintlock rifle and  pointed at the men and exclaimed, "Come on gentle, but I'll 
2 CONC promise that one of you will bite the dust."  The men left.  John returned that night.  The family left the next morning without seeing any of the men that had threatened Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1846 Mary and her family moved across the Mississippi where they lived in tents until the end of August when they moved to Winter Quarters.  They stopped in Des Moines because of illness.  Here on the 18 August 1846 five y
2 CONC ear old Elizabeth died.  When they arrived in Mt. Pisgah their five month old William M. died 16 September 1846.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 16, 1846 Hyrum started back to visit his grandmother and drowned crossing the icy river.  John and Mary traveled with John Taylor wagon train.  They arrived in the Salt Lake Valley 30 September 1847.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 February 1849, John was appointed Bishop of the Second Ward in Salt Lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1849 they moved to Manti, Utah.  Mary sprained her ankle at the camp of Salt Creek Canyon.  On 22 November 1849 they arrived in Manti.  At first they lived in tents but heavy snow and intense cold forced them to live in
2 CONC  caves.  In the spring hundreds of rattlesnakes appeared in the caves where they were living.  In 1850 John was called to be a Bishop of the Manti Ward.  Mary's mother came to Manti to live with her until she died.  Mary died at a
2 CONC ge 57; 11 November 1859.  She is buried in the Manti Cemetery.
2 CONT
