1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} CLESSON, Matthew (-1715, Deerfield) & Mary PHELPS (1651-1687); 22 Dec 1670; Northampton {Northampton (ms) 144, 253; Scott (1919) 25; Reg. 3:176, 90:160; Deerfield; Sv. 1:408; Warner-Harrington 502 has Closson} [New
2 CONC  England Marriages to 1700, Torrey, NEHGS CD 2001]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Mathew_Clesson_(2)<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew was born in Ireland abt 1649.  Matthew Clesson was an Irishman, and according to Savage "he was a servant in Northampton, and was probably indentured like the others".  He took the oath of Allegiance on the 8 Feb. 1678. In
2 CONC  1668 he signed a petition against imposts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trumbull continues: "At that time, little sympathy was wasted by the pioneers of Northampton upon the Irish. Willing that natives of the Emerald Isle should become residents, lands were granted to them on conditions expressly proh
2 CONC ibiting them from gaining citizenship thereby." He was granted three acres, "but not as a hom lote". He later became quite prosperous, and at one point owned "59 acres of land, lying in 12 different parcels, all of which, with the
2 CONC  exception of seven acres and sixty rods, he purchased".  He bought the homestead of Thomas Lewis in 1667 and sold it to Solomon Stoddard in 1684. In 1672-3, he contributed to the support of Harvard College. He married Mary Phelps
2 CONC . Northampton VR's have marriage date of 22 Dec 1670. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His dwelling house was burned by the Indians in 1675 (King Philip's War), and the town made him other grants in compensation for his losses. He married Mary Phelps, d/o Deacon Nathaniel Phelps in 1670, and eight children were born
2 CONC  to them in Northampton. At some time about 1686, they moved to Deerfield, where Mary soon died. After the death of his first wife in 1687, he married (2) 21 Nov. 1701, Sarah Hedge, and at the time he made his Will, his wife was n
2 CONC amed Susannah.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Queen Anne's French and Indian War, a Matthew Clesson joined the scouting expedition of Capt. Benjamin Wright in 1709. (I feel that this was probably Matthew Clesson Jr. - his son). The expedition surprised the enemy near L
2 CONC ake Champlain, and routed them. De Rouville and 180 French & Indians started toward the English settlements on June 22nd seeking revenge. Trumbull states: "they were discovered in the vicinity of Deerfield, and driven off, having 
2 CONC succeeded in capturing Joseph Clesson and John Arms. In the skirmish, Jonathan Williams was killed, and Matthew Clesson mortally wounded." Matthew died 1716 in Deerfield, Franklin, MA, at 67 years of age.
