1 NAME Nicolas /De La Vergne/ Sr
2 GIVN Nicolas
2 SURN De La Vergne
2 NSFX Sr
2 _MARNM De La Vergne
2 NICK Nicholas
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Washington Hollow, Dutchess County, New York
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1697
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint-Priest
3 STAE Rhone-Alpes
3 CTRY France
1 DEAT
2 DATE APR 1782
2 ADDR
3 CITY Millbrook
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Came to U.S. in 1718
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas De La Vergne left a will on 6 June 1780 in Charlotte, Dutchess, New York. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Silas Deuel \ Duval, Mary De La Vergne and Mary Husted were mentioned in the will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas De La Vergne died in April 1782 in Charlotte, Dutchess, New York. Nicholas's will was proved on 8 April 1782 in Charlotte, Dutchess, New York; the will mentions: wife Mary; sons Giles, Joseph, Nicholas, James, Ebenezer, W
2 CONC alter; daughters Mary, Sarah, Elisabeth, Hannah, Susanna. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of New York State, Lewis Historical Publishing Company, Inc., 1927 This book is owned by Pam Rietsch and is a part of the Mardos Memorial Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name De La Vergne was written among those of the American colonists in the year preceding the Revolutionary War by Nicholas De la Vergne, a physician, who was born in France in 1702, came to America in 1722, and during the lat
2 CONC ter years of his life was Probate Judge of Dutchess County. He died in 1782. His son, Benjamin De La Vergne, also a physician, gave his service to the patriot army in this war for freedom, serving as a major with the Fourth Regime
2 CONC nt of the new York Militia, and was a member of the Third Provisional Congress held in New York City. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Geneological Outline of the DeLaVergne Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Since it's founding in America in 1720
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of Arms--Silver field, green figures, uprooted tree in center, rampant lion on right of tree, naked sword to left of tree
2 CONT
2 CONT Crest-- Running lion under crown with seven jewels
2 CONT
2 CONT Motto-- "Esto Semper Fidelis (Thou shall be faithful always)
2 CONT
2 CONT   The first of the DeLaVergne (or delavergne) in America was one Nicholas who was born in the province of Auvergne in 1698.  The chateau or castle in which he was born still exists and is used by certain members of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Nicholas left France while still a medical student.  Banished from his fatherland because of political troubles, he hastily accepted a proffered postition on a French man-of-war sailing for the new world and arriving in America
2 CONC , he left the ship and settled down in New York state in 1720.  It is not known definitely where he first established his home.  
2 CONT
2 CONT    First public mention of the doctor is found in the register of deeds at Poughkeepsie in papers concerning various land operations which show that at that time he was a man of some wealth.  On May 27, 1749 he is named as Nichola
2 CONC s DeLaVergne,Gentleman, in a deed given by Joshua Hunt of Westchester for 206 acres of land in lot twelve in the lower nine Partner's section.  This piece of land was his home for the rest of his life.  Here a built the substantia
2 CONC l home which is standing in fine condition today on part of the large estate of Okligh Thorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT    By his first wife he had three children, Louis, Benjamin and Frances.  By his second there were eleven children.
2 CONT
2 CONT    The following is taken from the Dutchess County History. "In 1747 he as on his farm which now forms a portion of the Okligh Thorne place in the town of Washington.  In the supervision books of that year he is allowed one pound,
2 CONC  shilling for pasturing twelve horses and serving the assessers.  He was a man of affairs, justice of peace and probate judge.  He speculated in numerous land transactions and practiced his profession.  In 1748 he assessed thirtee
2 CONC n pounds, ten shillings which shows he was a man of substance.  He was know throughout the country as the "French Doctor" and a dam which he built is know today as the "Little French  doctor's dam".  He died in 1783.  He took the 
2 CONC oath of allegiance to George III in 1761.
2 CONT
2 CONT   He was probate judge for Dutchess county for a number of years.  Documents covering this period may be found in the Dutchess County History and English Documents at Albany, Volume II, Page 100.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Deeds covering a period of thirty years show Nicholas to have been engaged in many real estate transactions expecially in Charlotte precinct and Parkman's Thact.  
2 CONT
2 CONT     The above data and geneological record were furnished me in 1928 by the mother of Paul Mason DeLaVergne who graduated from the University of Michigan in 1931.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                        Earl DeLaVergne
2 CONT --------------------
