1 OCCU Owner and Editor of Pittsburgh Dispatch Newspaper
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mr. DANIEL O'NEILL, proprietor of the Pittsburg (
2 CONT
2 CONT United States) " Despatch" newspaper, died, 1877. Mr.
2 CONT
2 CONT O'Neill was a native of the County Wexford, being born at
2 CONT
2 CONT Cloughbawn, barony of Ban try, on New Year's Day, 1830.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Mr. Hugh O'Neill, was principal of a school
2 CONT
2 CONT there, which had been established by the grandfather of
2 CONT
2 CONT the present Lord Carew, and from which emanated some
2 CONT
2 CONT good scholars and successful writers. The subject of our
2 CONT
2 CONT present notice was the eldest of twelve children, and at a
2 CONT
2 CONT very early age showed a marked taste for journalism, becoming
2 CONT
2 CONT a frequent contributor to the " Wexford Independent."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851 he emigrated to America, and at once
2 CONT
2 CONT proceeded West to Pittsburg, where he secured an engagement,
2 CONT
2 CONT at a very small salary, from Colonel J. Heron Foster,
2 CONT
2 CONT proprietor of the " Despatch," as reporter on that journal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here he remained for some time, and in 1856 accepted the
2 CONT
2 CONT position of local editor of the " Chronicle," published in
2 CONT
2 CONT the same city. He continued to write for this paper until
2 CONT
2 CONT the war broke out, when he went South as its war correspondent,
2 CONT
2 CONT and also a member of a Sanitary Committee from
2 CONT
2 CONT Pittsburg. In 1865, lie purchased a share in the " Despatch,"
2 CONT
2 CONT the paper he was first engaged on. We cannot
2 CONT
2 CONT conclude this notice better than by quoting the words of
2 CONT
2 CONT the American Papers when noticing the death of Mr.
2 CONT
2 CONT O'Neill : — " No more convincing evidence of his indomitable
2 CONT
2 CONT energy could be adduced than that afforded in the success
2 CONT
2 CONT of the " Despatch." The paper had been run down
2 CONT
2 CONT for want of attention, but under the direction of the new
2 CONT
2 CONT firm it rapidly recovered the ground, and has reached its
2 CONT
2 CONT present successful condition. He turned a pigmy sheet
2 CONT
2 CONT into a powerful giant, made his name a household word in
2 CONT
2 CONT Pittsburg, and his paper a welcome visitor. As a writer,
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. O'Neill possessed what is usually termed a trenchant
2 CONT
2 CONT pen. He was quick, incisive, and if occasion served, bitter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was aggressive rather than inviting, and when in
2 CONT
2 CONT municipal affairs any scheme lacked his approbation, the
2 CONT
2 CONT public were sure to find it out, and that very soon. He
2 CONT
2 CONT was by no means a polished writer, though his education
2 CONT
2 CONT was classical and his reading varied. His unpolished sentences
2 CONT
2 CONT were wonderfully vigorous, however, and he may be
2 CONT
2 CONT said to have possessed a style almost incapable of imitation.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his active editorial life he mingled somewhat in
2 CONT
2 CONT politics, and for several terms sat in the City Councils. —
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the last Electoral College of Pennsylvania,
2 CONT
2 CONT and the last public act of his life was to cast his
2 CONT
2 CONT ballot for Hayes and Wheeler, at Harrisburg, on the 7th
2 CONT
2 CONT December, 1876, when the Electoral College met. During
2 CONT
2 CONT his residence of a little over a quarter of a century he has
2 CONT
2 CONT amassed a handsome competency. A year or two since he
2 CONT
2 CONT completed the spacious residence on Penn Avenue, known
2 CONT
2 CONT as ' Linden House,' where he and his family have since
2 CONT
2 CONT resided. He came here poor and leaves his wife and children
2 CONT
2 CONT a large fortune." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronicles of the County Wexford: Being a Record of Memorable Incidents, Disasters, Social Occurrences, and Crimes, Also, Biographies of Eminent Persons, &c., &c., Brought Down to the Year 1877
2 CONT
2 CONT By George Griffiths, of County Wexford George Griffiths
2 CONT
2 CONT Published by printed at the "Watchman" office, 1890
2 CONT
2 CONT  Daniiel O'Neill was born on new years day 1830 the eldest of twelve children. He was son of Hugh O'Neill who taught Patrick Kennedy in the schoolhouse in Cloughbawn. He like his father became a teacher. He had a drink problem and
2 CONC  was dismissed from Cloughbawn National School and emegrated to America. He went to Pittsburg Penn where he secured a job as a reporter from Col J Hernon Foster who was propritor of the Pittsburg Dispatch with Reece C Fleeson. Bot
2 CONC h men were propritors, publishers and editors and their office was on 4th street. The Dispatch was first published on 8th February 1846 and by 1851 it had a circulation of 15,000 copies.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The Dispatch shared the forth floor of the building with another Pittsburg paper the Evening Chronicle which was published by John B Kennedy. After five years with the Dispatch O'Neill changed over to The Chronicle to become it's
2 CONC  city editor.
2 CONT
2 CONT  When the American Civil War broke out O'Neill moved south to become war corrispondent. After the war O'Neill purchased shares in the Dispatch and later gained ownership with a collogue from the Chronicle Alexander W Rooke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel O'Neill entered politics and sat on the Pittsburg City Council for several years. He lived in Lynden House on Penn Avenue which was completed one year before his death in 1877. The Pittsburg Dispatch ceased publication on 8
2 CONC th February 1923
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.esatclear.ie/~lorcand/history.htm#O%27Neill
