1 NOTE {geni:about_me} O'NEILL, Eugene M., journalist, was born in
2 CONT
2 CONT Clonroche. County Wexford, Ireland, Sept. 10, 1850,
2 CONT
2 CONT son of Hugh O'Neill, a well-known educator, and
2 CONT
2 CONT one of the founders of the system
2 CONT
2 CONT of nalional education in Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The boy was educated by his father,
2 CONT
2 CONT and afterward at the training college,
2 CONT
2 CONT Dublin, from which he was
2 CONT
2 CONT graduated at the age of seventeen,
2 CONT
2 CONT lie came to the United States in
2 CONT
2 CONT March, 1867. His brother, Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT O'Neill, was publisher and editor
2 CONT
2 CONT of the Piltsburg "Dispatch," and
2 CONT
2 CONT the younger brother found work
2 CONT
2 CONT on the paper as reporter. Under
2 CONT
2 CONT the tutelage of his brother he soon
2 CONT
2 CONT acquired a thorough knowledge
2 CONT
2 CONT of editorial work, and was, until
2 CONT
2 CONT 1874, an editorial writer on the "
2 CONT
2 CONT Dispatch." He then devoted
2 CONT
2 CONT himself to the studv of law, and
2 CONT
2 CONT was duly admitted to the bar
2 CONT
2 CONT and practiced in the courts of
2 CONT
2 CONT Allegheny county. After his brother's death he
2 CONT
2 CONT took the management of the " Dispatch, "and in January,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1877, became the publisher of that well-known
2 CONT
2 CONT and influential journal. Mr. O'Neill was elected n
2 CONT
2 CONT select councilman of the city of Pittsburg in 1875,
2 CONT
2 CONT the only political office he ever accepted. 
2 CONT
2 CONT O'NEILL, Eugene M. :
2 CONT
2 CONT Editor; born In Wexford, Ireland, in
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850. He received a university education
2 CONT
2 CONT in Ireland, and came to Pittsburgh,
2 CONT
2 CONT where his brother Daniel was editor
2 CONT
2 CONT and proprietor of The Dispatch. He
2 CONT
2 CONT married in Pittsburgh, Emma, widow
2 CONT
2 CONT of Daniel O'Neill. He studied law, was
2 CONT
2 CONT admitted to the bar and engaged in practice.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of his brother Daniel
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1877, he took charge of The Dispatch,
2 CONT
2 CONT which, under his management, became
2 CONT
2 CONT the paper of largest circulation and influence
2 CONT
2 CONT in Pittsburgh. lie continued as
2 CONT
2 CONT editor and publisher of the paper until
2 CONT
2 CONT 1902. when he retired from active management,
2 CONT
2 CONT remaining, however, vice-president
2 CONT
2 CONT and a stockholder in the Dispatch
2 CONT
2 CONT Publishing Company, and is also a director
2 CONT
2 CONT in manufacturing corporations and
2 CONT
2 CONT a large owner of real estate. Address:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pen n and Linden Avenues, Pittsburgh. 
