1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * William's birth, professional, and death information are available in his obituary in ''The Dispatch'', Warwick, N.Y., April 1907 [the banner of the page is not visible. From other newspapers, it was ascertained 
2 CONC that William died on Monday, 1 April 1907.] 
2 CONT ** "Mr. Dunning married Miss Clara Frost of New Orleans. They have six daughters, Ruth, Marceline, Clara, Belle, Elsie and Wilhelmina.  
2 CONT ** "For several years Mr. Dunning and family have occupied for the greater part of the year a fine summer home near Warwick, the old Belcher farm, at Stone Bridge, where Mr. Dunning delighted to spend his leisure time, enjoying th
2 CONC e simple country life. He was a man devoid of all ostentation, of a most cordial and kindly nature, and much beloved by a wide circle of friends, and by none was he held in higher esteem than by his farmer neighbors in the Warwick
2 CONC  Valley, who regard his untimely death a personal loss." 
2 CONT * Also see https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/155802723/william-fullerton-dunning
2 CONT * From https://www.gutenberg.org/files/49260/49260-h/49260-h.htm:
2 CONT ** "WILLIAM FULLERTON DUNNING, a member of the New York City Bar Association and for several years immediately preceding his death a member of its committee on admissions, died on April 1, 1907, after an illness lasting only a few
2 CONC  days. He was born in the city of New York on May 29, 1856. His father was the late Benjamin F. Dunning, for many years the law partner of Charles O'Conor, and his mother was Ruth Seely, of Orange County, New York.
2 CONT ** "From his father Mr. Dunning inherited a predilection for the law, and doubtless his early association with his father's firm helped to develop in him his high ideals of professional ethics.
2 CONT ** "He was prepared for college in Dresden, Saxony, and was graduated from Princeton University in the class of 1877. He devoted himself with more than usual assiduity to his studies, and was particularly interested in the classic
2 CONC s. He was graduated from the Columbia College Law School and admitted to the bar of this State in 1879. Upon his admission to the bar he entered the firm of Dunning, Edsall, Hart & Fowler, of which firm his father was senior partn
2 CONC er. In 1883, upon the death of Mr. Hart, the firm of Dunning, Edsall & Fowler was organized, and in 1886, upon the retirement of Mr. Edsall, the firm became Dunning & Fowler. Of these three firms William Fullerton Dunning was a me
2 CONC mber. In 1900, upon the dissolution of the firm of Dunning & Fowler, Mr. Dunning joined the firm of Boardman, Platt & Soley; on Mr. Soley's retirement from the firm in 1906, the firm Boardman, Platt & Dunning was organized and con
2 CONC tinued until November, 1906, when Judge Morgan J. O'Brien became one of the members, the name being changed to O'Brien, Boardman, Platt & Dunning.
2 CONT ** "William Fullerton Dunning was a man of modest and retiring disposition. Although a learned and sound lawyer, he did not seek the contention and strife of the courts, but preferred office practice. His faithfulness and devotion
2 CONC  to his clients' interests gained for him their confidence, and many of them became his warm friends. He had a cheerful, happy disposition. His relations with his professional brethren were always marked with great consideration, 
2 CONC and while he jealously guarded the interests of his clients, an opposing attorney could never justly complain that Mr. Dunning had not shown to him the courtesy demanded by the most exacting professional etiquette. While not ambit
2 CONC ious for position or office which would bring him prominently before the public, he was greatly interested in his professional work and desirous that it should be well done. His relations with the various partners with whom he was
2 CONC  associated during his career have always been most cordial and friendly.
2 CONT ** "In 1883 he married Clara Frost, of New Orleans, La., and the years that followed were filled with domestic happiness. His wife and six daughters who survive him mourn the loss of a kind and loving husband and a wise and affect
2 CONC ionate father. His life is a continuous record of high ideals and work well done. He died in the prime of life, respected by all who knew him, and loved by those who knew him best. In this age of excitement and greed, such a life 
2 CONC as his should be an example and an inspiration."
2 CONT
