1 NAME Daniel Dean /Buckles/
2 GIVN Daniel Dean
2 _MARNM Buckles
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Silvercreek
3 STAE OH
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 SEP 1834
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 JAN 1900
2 ADDR
3 CITY Jamestown
3 STAE OH
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Saddle and harness Maker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Observation by Todd Barber, "I found these listed together by coincidence since they are alphabetical but I believe this John D Bryan may be the one that donated land to create John Bryan State Park.  We have famil
2 CONC y lore that we are related to that person but there are so many John Bryans it's been difficult to find out which one..this may be a clue" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bryan, John D., Pvt., Company C, 60th Regiment, Infantry, Ross Twp Buckles, Daniel D., Pvt., Company A, 74th Regiment, Infantry, Silvercreek Twp (Cem)
2 CONT
2 CONT Served Ohio Enlisted A Co. 74th Inf Reg. OH disch disability at Jeffersonville, IN on 11 May 1865 Source: Official Roster of the Soldiers of the State of Ohio Abbreviation: OHRoster Published by on 1886 
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Dean Buckles was  a saddle and harness maker.   He was a veteran of the Civil War Symbols on his ornate grave point to him being a member of the Independent Order of Odd Fellows (A fraternal organization) He was also likely
2 CONC  a member of Woodmen of the World because a Tree Trunk/Stump usually marks the graves of Woodmen of the World members. Also, they usually contain other symbols such as anchors, lilies, vines, etc. Broken braches on the tree symbol
2 CONC ize a life cut short.  This was an early benefit of Woodmen of the World membership, and they are found in cemeteries nationwide. This program was abandoned in the late 1920s as too costly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Typically the headstones would include a depiction of the WOW relics—symbols of the organization. These include most notably a stump or felled tree (inscribed into a more generic monument in some cases, rather than the more notice
2 CONC able instances of the entire monument being in the shape of the log or tree-stump); the maul and wedge; an axe; and often a Dove of Peace with an olive branch. As Woodmen "do not lie" a common inscription: "Here rests a Woodman of
2 CONC  the World".
2 CONT
2 CONT According to family legend (Gail Little said) we had ancestor's on the Yarnell side that fought with Sherman on that Great March, but he never told me WHO. I looked and looked for the name Yarnell many times, but (knokw) I know it
2 CONC  is Daniel Dean Buckles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the Seventy-Fourth, Company A under Captain Robert Hunter as a PVT. Buckles                Daniel          D.    Sep 10, 1834     Jan 25, 1900       66   -             Silvercreek Old                Civil War       
2 CONT
2 CONT He was listed as Discharged (but not under discharged by expiration of service)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordered to Camp Chase, Ohio, February 24, 1862, and duty there until April 20. Moved to Nashville, Tenn., April 20–24. Dumont's Expedition over the Cumberland Mountains, Tenn., June 1862. Guard duty along railroad between Nashvill
2 CONC e and Columbia, Tenn., until September 3. Siege of Nashville September 12-November 7. Fort Riley near Nashville October 5. Gallatin Pike near Nashville October 20. Duty at Nashville until December 26. Advance on Murfreesboro Decem
2 CONC ber 26–30. Battle of Stones River December 30–31, 1862 and January 1–3, 1863. Duty at Murfreesboro until June. Tullahoma Campaign June 23-July 7. Occupation of middle Tennessee until August 16. Passage of the Cumberland Mountains 
2 CONC and Tennessee River and Chickamauga Campaign August 16-September 22. Davis Cross Roads or Dug Gap September 11. Battle of Chickamauga September 19–21. Rossville Gap September 21. Siege of Chattanooga, Tenn., September 24-November 
2 CONC 23. Chattanooga-Ringgold Campaign November 23–27. Orchard Knob November 23–24. Missionary Ridge November 25. Regiment reenlisted January 1, 1864. Veterans on furlough January 25-April 12. Atlanta Campaign May 1-September 8. Demons
2 CONC tration on Rocky Faced Ridge May 8–11. Buzzard's Roost Gap May 8–9. Battle of Resaca May 14–15. Mill Springs Gap May 19. Operations on line of Pumpkin Vine Creek and battles about Dallas, New Hope Church and Allatoona Hills May 25
2 CONC -June 5. Pickett's Mills May 27. Operations about Marietta and against Kennesaw Mountain June 10-July 2. Pine Hill June 11–14. Lost Mountain June 15–17. Assault on Kennesaw June 27. Ruff's Station July 4. Chattahoochie River July 
2 CONC 5–17. Peachtree Creek July 19–20. Siege of Atlanta July 22-August 25. Utoy Creek August 5–7. Flank movement on Jonesboro August 25–30. Battle of Jonesboro August 31-September 1. Operations against Hood in northern Georgia and nort
2 CONC hern Alabama September 29-November 3. March to the sea November 15-December 10. Siege of Savannah December 10–21. Campaign of the Carolinas January to April 1865. Taylor's Hole Creek, Averysboro, N. C., March 16. Battle of Bentonv
2 CONC ille March 19–21. Occupation of Goldsboro March 24. Advance on Raleigh April 10–14. Occupation of Raleigh April 14. Bennett's House April 26. Surrender of Johnston and his army. March to Washington, D.C., via Richmond, Va., April 
2 CONC 29-May 20. Grand Review May 24. Moved to Louisville, Ky., June, and duty there until July. Casualties
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment lost a total of 164 men during service; 2 officers and 51 enlisted men killed or mortally wounded, 2 officers and 105 enlisted men died of disease.
2 CONT ===============
2 CONT   Keith Little    February 25 at 3:11pm   Report
2 CONT It looks so much like a real tree this time of year that I did the triple take. Daniel Buckles had a daughter named Immogene Buckles 1866 -1905 she is burried right behind him (east). To the right (south) of Daniel's tombstone is 
2 CONC Jennie L. Buckles 1847 to 1930. Near the Sutton plot which is to the right (south). Preston Glass died March 6, 1903 age 83 his wife Suzanna Glass died Sept 8, 1886 aged 67 years 3 months. They are burried in front of D.D. Buckles
2 CONC  grave. 
