1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Claypole
2 CONT
2 CONT John Claypole (21 August 1625 – 26 June 1688), was an officer in the Parliamentary army in 1645 during the English Civil War. He was created Lord Cleypole by Oliver Cromwell, but this title naturally came to an end with the Restor
2 CONC ation of 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claypole married Elizabeth, Oliver Cromwell's second daughter, before October 1646, and raised a troop of horse for Parliament to oppose Charles II in 1651. He was master of the horse to his father-in-law the Lord Protector. A Mem
2 CONC ber of Parliament in 1654 and 1656, he was one of Cromwell's peers in 1657. After the restoration of the monarchy he lived quietly, but may have been briefly imprisoned as a suspect in a plot in 1678, only to be released when no e
2 CONC vidence of his involvement was presented.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Cleypole, 1st Bt. was admitted to Gray's Inn on 30 June 1651. He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Carmarthenshire from 1654 to 1655. He was commander of the a Troop of Horse to oppose King Charles II's m
2 CONC arch in England. He held the office of a Lord of the Bedchamber to the Protector.  He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Northamptonshire between 1656 and 1658.  He was invested as a Knight on 16 July 1657.  He was
2 CONC  created 1st Baronet Cleypole [England] on 20 July 1657, created by Oliver, Lord Protector. After 1660 his title was disallowed after the Restoration. On 5 June 1660 he signed a declaration of allegiance to the King.  He lived at 
2 CONC Norborough, Peterborough, Northamptonshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT another source gives John and Elizabeth Cromwell
2 CONT
2 CONT marriage date as september 1646
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
