1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''James Claypoole, 1634-1687, an admired friend of William Penn, the Quaker. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigrated to America (Philadelphia, PA) in 1683; Member of Colonial Assembly 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT James and Helena attended William Penn's wedding in 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Claypoole-5
2 CONT
2 CONT James Claypool was married to Helena Mercer at Bremen, Germany, 12 February 1657/8 by Conradus Leilus, a Calvinist Minister. They had ten sons and four daughters.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Claypoole, born in London on Nicholas Lane in 15 November 1658.[1][16]
2 CONT # James Claypoole, born 12 August 1664 in Scots Yard near London Stone.[17][16][18][1].
2 CONT # Nathaniel Claypoole (first of that name), born 23 September 1668 at the Sign of the Stille Upon Horsely Down in Southwark.[16]died on 14 November 1668.[19][1]
2 CONT # Josiah Claypoole, born 9 November 1669 in Scots Yard, London. He died 2 May 1670 at Kingston upon Thames and was buried there at the Friends' Burying Ground.[16][1]
2 CONT # Samuel Claypoole, born 19 March 1670 in Scots Yard, London, England.[20][1]Died March 1680 in London.[21][16]
2 CONT # Nathaniel Claypoole (second of that name), born 4 October 1672 in Scots Yard, London.[16][22][1]
2 CONT # George Claypoole, born 13 Jan 1674 in Scots Yard, London.[16][23][1]
2 CONT # Unnamed stillborn son, born between January and March in 1673.[16][1]
2 CONT # Joseph Claypoole (first of that name), born 29 March 1676 in Scots Yard, London.[16] Died 30 August 1676 at Lambeth, England and buried in a Friends' Burying Ground by Moorfields (Bunhill Fields).[24][1]
2 CONT # Joseph Claypoole (second of that name), born 14 July 1677 in Scots Yard, London.[16][25][1]
2 CONT # Mary Claypoole, wife of Francis Cooke.[1] Born 14 October 1660, Mincing Lane in London.[16][26]
2 CONT # Helena Claypoole, born 6 November 1662 in Scots Yard, London. She was the wife of William Bethel.[1][16][27]
2 CONT # Priscilla Claypoole, born 25 April 1666 in Scots Yard, London. She was the wife of Dr. John Crappe.[16][1]
2 CONT # Elizabeth Claypoole, born 25 July 1678 in Scots Yard, London. She passed away on 31 July 1678 and was buried at the Friends Burial Ground by Checker Alley, London (Bunhill Fields).[16][28][29][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Claypoole died testate on 6 August 1687.[1] He left his land and his silver to his wife and children. His daughters Mary, Helena and Priscilla received land, as did his sons Nathaniel, George and Joseph. He also left 25 bush
2 CONC els of Indian corn to the poor. William Penn was one of the Executors of the will.[30]
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT James Claypoole was a British merchant who was familiar with the boats in the harbor.There was an older Concord that sailed England to Barbadoes. This apparently was a newer version. "London the 6th, 12mo, 1682/83 (date old style 
2 CONC making this the month of Feb.) We are all inclined to go on this vessel now put up, she is a brave ship of 500 tons burden, and 26 guns, called the Concord, the master, William Jeffries, who has used long the Virginia trade and is
2 CONC  well acquainted with that voyage...." This comes from London Port books: Jeffries William, Concord, 450 or 500 tons, 40 guns, to Pennsylvania 1683. _______________________________________ Chester County Ships Listing for The Conc
2 CONC ord  _______ Here are the passengers listed as being aboard this ship.  Date of Arrival: 10/1683 
2 CONT #Master: William Jefferies
2 CONT # Lenert Aratts (Arents)
2 CONT # Elizabeth Bennett, servant to James Claypoole
2 CONT # Johannes Bleikers
2 CONT #James Claypoole and wife Helena and seven children
2 CONT #Edward Cole, Jr, servant to Claypool
2 CONT # William Hard
2 CONT # Peter Keurlis
2 CONT # Thones Kunder
2 CONT # Hugh Lamb
2 CONT # Jan Lensen
2 CONT # Jan Luykens
2 CONT # Hugh Masland and wife, servant to Claypoole
2 CONT #Abraham Op Den Graeff
2 CONT #Derick Op Den Graeff
2 CONT # Hermann Op Den Graeff
2 CONT # Jan Siemes
2 CONT # Willem Streypers
2 CONT # Leonard Teison (Tyson)
2 CONT # Reyner Teissen
2 CONT # Abraham Tunes
2 CONT # Cicely Wooley, servant to Claypoole
2 CONT
2 CONT CLAYPOOLE, James, pioneer, born in 1634; died in August, 1686. He was the fifth son of John Claypoole, and his wife Mary, daughter of William Angell, of London. His brother John married the daughter of Oliver Cromwell. James becam
2 CONC e a Quaker, and was an intimate friend of William Penn. He was a merchant in London, and witnessed the signing of the Charter of Privileges granted by Penn to the settlers in 1682. First signature on Charter of Pennsylvania after 
2 CONC William Penn. He was the treasurer of the Free society of traders, which was formed to assist in the settlement of Pennsylvania, and, after attending to its affairs in England, he immigrated with his family, in 1683, to Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia, where he held important offices.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to PA having previously secured 5,000 acres of land from William Penn (October 1681) and settled a Colony at Germantown, PA. He was Treasurer of the "Free Society of Traders of PA; Justice of the Peace; Judge in the Provincia
2 CONC l Court; member of the Governor's Council and also of the Assembly of PA, and in 1686 was Register-General of PA. He resided in Phildelphia, and was a prominent merchant-citizen of June 8, 1683. He left a will in record book numbe
2 CONC r 8, page 110. Source: Sonja Vaughn & "Pepin The Frank to Harry St. Clair Walker of PA".
2 CONT
2 CONT "We set Saile from gravesend the 25 5mo 1683 and Arrived at philadelphia in pensylvania 8 8 mo 1683. we came in the ship called the Concord Captain Jeffrys Commder burthen 550 tuhn."<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ''~•Source: Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families From the Pennsylvania Magazine of History and Biography. Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc. Baltimore, MD 1983 p 178.'' Dear Ancestor  ''Your tombstone stands among the rest;''<br/>
2 CONT   ''neglected and alone'' <br/>
2 CONT ''The name and date are chiseled out'' <br/>
2 CONT ''on polished, marbled stone'' <br/>
2 CONT ''It reaches out to all who care'' <br/>
2 CONT ''It is too late to mourn'' <br/>
2 CONT ''You did not know that I exist'' <br/>
2 CONT ''You died and I was born.''<br/>
2 CONT   ''Yet each of us are cells of you'' <br/>
2 CONT ''in flesh, in blood, in bone.'' <br/>
2 CONT ''Our blood contracts and beats a pulse'' <br/>
2 CONT ''entirely not our own.'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===aside===
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Ancestor, the place you filled 
2 CONT one hundred years ago  Spreads out among the ones you left  who would have loved you so.  I wonder if you lived and loved,  I wonder if you knew  That someday I would find this spot,  and come to visit you. 
2 CONT  Author -Unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT •James and Helena attended William Penn's wedding in 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Came to PA having previously secured 5,000 acres of land from William Penn (October 1681)  and settled a Colony at Germantown, PA.  He was Treasurer of the "Free Society of Traders of PA; Justice of the Peace; Judge in the Provin
2 CONC cial Court; member of the Governor's Council and also of the Assembly of PA, and in 1686 was Register-General of PA. He resided in Phildelphia, and was a prominent merchant-citizen of June 8, 1683. He left a will in record book nu
2 CONC mber 8, page 110. ''~Source: Sonja Vaughn & "Pepin The Frank to Harry St. Clair Walker of PA''
2 CONT
2 CONT "We set Saile from gravesend the 25 5mo 1683 and Arrived at philadelphia in pensylvania 8 8 mo 1683.  we came in the ship called the Concord Captain Jeffrys Commder burthen 550 tuhn." Source: Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families F
2 CONC rom the Pennsylvania Magazine of History and Biography.  Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc. Baltimore, MD 1983 p 178.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===aside===
2 CONT
2 CONT James Claypoole is my gggggggg-grandfather and I am very happy that I can claim that fact. An amazing adventure and so much history. Thank you grandfather for helping to build our nation.
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=8341003 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 23 2016, 5:57:13 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT James and Helena attended William Penn's wedding in 1672.
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT James Claypoole was a British merchant who was familiar with the boats in the harbor. There was an older Concord that sailed England to Barbadoes. This apparently was a newer version. "London the 6th, 12mo, 1682/83 (date old style
2 CONC  making this the month of Feb.) We are all inclined to go on this vessel now put up, she is a brave ship of 500 tons burden, and 26 guns, called the Concord, the master, William Jeffries, who has used long the Virginia trade and i
2 CONC s well acquainted with that voyage...."
2 CONT This comes from London Port books: Jeffries William, Concord, 450 or 500 tons, 40 guns, to Pennsylvania 1683.
2 CONT _______________________________________
2 CONT Chester County Ships Listing for The Concord
2 CONT _______
2 CONT Here are the passengers listed as being aboard this ship.
2 CONT Date of Arrival: 10/1683
2 CONT Master: William Jefferies
2 CONT Lenert Aratts (Arents)
2 CONT Elizabeth Bennett, servant to James Claypoole
2 CONT Johannes Bleikers
2 CONT James Claypoole and wife Helena and seven children
2 CONT Edward Cole, Jr, servant to Claypool
2 CONT William Hard
2 CONT Peter Keurlis
2 CONT Thones Kunder
2 CONT Hugh Lamb
2 CONT Jan Lensen
2 CONT Jan Luykens
2 CONT Hugh Masland and wife, servant to Claypoole
2 CONT Abraham Op Den Graeff
2 CONT Derick Op Den Graeff
2 CONT Hermann Op Den Graeff
2 CONT Jan Siemes
2 CONT Willem Streypers
2 CONT Leonard Teison (Tyson)
2 CONT Reyner Teissen
2 CONT Abraham Tunes
2 CONT Cicely Wooley, servant to Claypoole
2 CONT ______________________________________________________
2 CONT CLAYPOOLE, James, pioneer, born in 1634; died in August, 1686. He was the fifth son of John Claypoole, and his wife Mary, daughter of William Angell, of London. His brother John married the daughter of Oliver Cromwell. James becam
2 CONC e a Quaker, and was an intimate friend of William Penn. He was a merchant in London, and witnessed the signing of the Charter of Privileges granted by Penn to the settlers in 1682. First signature on Charter of Pennsylvania after 
2 CONC William Penn. He was the treasurer of the Free society of traders, which was formed to assist in the settlement of Pennsylvania, and, after attending to its affairs in England, he immigrated with his family, in 1683, to Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia, where he held important offices.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to PA having previously secured 5,000 acres of land from William Penn (October 1681) and settled a Colony at Germantown, PA. He was Treasurer of the "Free Society of Traders of PA; Justice of the Peace; Judge in the Provincia
2 CONC l Court; member of the Governor's Council and also of the Assembly of PA, and in 1686 was Register-General of PA. He resided in Phildelphia, and was a prominent merchant-citizen of June 8, 1683. He left a will in record book numbe
2 CONC r 8, page 110.
2 CONT Source: Sonja Vaughn & "Pepin The Frank to Harry St. Clair Walker of PA".
2 CONT
2 CONT "We set Saile from gravesend the 25 5mo 1683 and Arrived at philadelphia in pensylvania 8 8 mo 1683. we came in the ship called the Concord Captain Jeffrys Commder burthen 550 tuhn."
2 CONT Source: Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families From the Pennsylvania Magazine of History and Biography. Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc. Baltimore, MD 1983 p 178.
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT Dear Ancestor
2 CONT Your tombstone stands among the rest;
2 CONT neglected and alone
2 CONT The name and date are chiseled out
2 CONT on polished, marbled stone
2 CONT It reaches out to all who care
2 CONT It is too late to mourn
2 CONT You did not know that I exist
2 CONT You died and I was born.
2 CONT Yet each of us are cells of you
2 CONT in flesh, in blood, in bone.
2 CONT Our blood contracts and beats a pulse
2 CONT entirely not our own.
2 CONT Dear Ancestor, the place you filled
2 CONT one hundred years ago
2 CONT Spreads out among the ones you left
2 CONT who would have loved you so.
2 CONT I wonder if you lived and loved,
2 CONT I wonder if you knew
2 CONT That someday I would find this spot,
2 CONT and come to visit you.
2 CONT Author -Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT
