1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT =John J Lowry, Jr=
2 CONT
2 CONT Private in the Utah Territory Militia during the Indian Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lowry, Jr., son of John Lowry and Mary Wilcox was born Jan. 31, 1829 in Lewis County, Missouri.  He came to Utah Sept. 30, 1847, in the Edward Hunter ox team company.  He married Sarah Jane Brown, Nov. 27, 1851, at Manti (dau
2 CONC ghter of James Polly Brown and Eunice Reesor, pioneers of July 29, 1847, Captain Higgins company of James Brown contingent, Mormon Battalion).  She was born Oct. 27, 1834, Greenville, Indiana.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children:  John, b. Oct. 3, 1852, m. Lorency Anderson, March 14, 1876;  
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hazard, b. Dec. 8, 1853, m. Maria B. Larson, March 8, 1877; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Jane, b. Sept. 22, 1855, m. James C. Reynolds, May 9, 1878; 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Brown, b. Dec. 21, 1857, m. Ellen Hansen, Nov. 11, 1879; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eunice b. April 16, 1860, m. Michael W. Molen; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olive, b. Sept.1, 1862, m. George Edward Anderson, May 30, 1888; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida May, b. July 30, 1865, m. Samuel H. Allen, June 8, 1892; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dora, b. Nov. 9, 1869, m. Clare William Reid, Oct. 9, 1895.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The family resided in Manti and Springville, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married second, Mary Ann Allen in March 1857, Salt Lake City (daughter of Daniel Allen and Mary Ann Maurice, pioneers ox team company).  She was born March 10, 1837, Kirtland, Ohio.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children:  Daniel Allen, b. Jan. 7, 1858, m. Emily Ludvigsen, Oct. 22, 1885; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Artemesia, b. March 30, 1860, m. Hyrum A. Nelson; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Bell, b. March 7, 1863, m. Samuel Singleton; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Evelyn, b. Oct. 23, 1865, m. Nathaniel Crawford, Oct. 22, 1885; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Diantha, b. March 31, 1868, m. William F. Reid; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Orson Hyrum, b. Aug. 23, 1873 (d. March 26, 1913), m. Matilda Eleanor Jensen, July 20, 1897.  Family home was also in Manti.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lowry, Jr., was a high priest and a  patriarch.  He settled at Manti in 1849.  He was a veteran of the Indian War.  Commissioned by Brigham Young, as paymaster in infantry of San Pete County 1855.  Missionary to Elk mountains
2 CONC , under President Alfred Billings, 1855.  Interpreter for Ute and Shoshone Indians.  Member of legislature from Manti 1986; also city councilman four terms, 1851-59, and assessor and clerk four years, and was also a Farmer an Stoc
2 CONC k Raiser.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death Certificate for John Lowry Jr.''' - https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000014139314840?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000082930744035
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT PUBLISHED IN MANTI MESSENGER November 12, 1915
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN LOWRY ANSWERS THE CALL--- John Lowry Jr., of Springville, member of the Nauvoo legion, and pioneer of 1847, explorer, Indian interpreter and peacemaker, died at the home of his eldest daughter, Mrs. S.L. Reynolds, in Salt Lak
2 CONC e City Sunday, after an illness of more than a year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years ago a train smashed into a wagon Mr. Lowry was driving, and he was badly shaken up, and last December, while boarding a street car in Salt Lake he suffered a broken collar bone. These two accidents are believed to have h
2 CONC astened his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lowry was born in Lewis County, Missouri, January 31, 1829. His early life was spent on his father's farm. He was a member of the Nauvoo Legion and came to Utah in September, 1847, with the John Taylor Company. He accompanied
2 CONC  his father to Manti in 1849 and was one of the first settlers to take up land in Sanpete County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the winter of 1849-1850 he was a member of the Pratt party, which explored southern Utah, and in 185 he was a leader of the Elk Mountain mission. In 1857 he accompanied President Brigham Young to the Salmon River country on
2 CONC  an exploring and settling expedition. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During his life on the frontier he gained a wide reputation as an Indian interpreter, and was frequently called upon to make peace in behalf of whites with hostile redskins. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Mr. Lowry was in school but a few months he acquired a wide education by observation and at the time of his death was one of the best informed men in the state on political and historical matters. During the latter part o
2 CONC f his life he did all he could to foster education and was well known as one of the state's most progressive citizens. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two wives and eleven children survive him. They are Mrs. Sarah Brown Lowry, of Salt Lake, Mrs. Mary Allen Lowry of Manti, John Lowry III of Manti, William B. Lowry of Arco, Idaho, Daniel A. Lowry of Ferron, Utah, Mrs. J.C. Reynold
2 CONC s of Salt Lake, Mrs. G. E. Anderson of Springville, Mrs. S.H. Allen of Salt Lake, Mrs. Ed. Olson of Prove, Mrs. Samuel Singleton of Ferron, Utah and Mrs. Nathaniel Crawford of Ferron, Utah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains were brought to this city Tuesday and the funeral held at the Tabernacle Wednesday, Bishop Jacobson presiding. Other speakers were Bishop Huntington of Springville and Bishop Peterson, Bishop Kjar, and Geo D. Bench.
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=18934286 Find A Grave Memorial] via daughter [http://www.geni.com/profile-34673576822 Eunice  Molen (born Lowry)] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy
2 CONC /18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 13 2015, 22:46:49 UTC''
2 CONT
