1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Per ShoalLakeHistory.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Travelers knew early Shoal Lake as a place with adequate lodgings, most of them clustered around the railway station, the major form of travel after 1885. Looming large and white against the prairie sky was Jubilee House on North 
2 CONC Railway, its impressive welcoming façade beckoning new arrivals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvin Westover built the Jubilee in the 1890s. The hotel was three storeys tall, wood frame with plenty of four paned rectangular windows and a central entrance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An elaborate and attractive porch spanned the first floor, its roof a balcony for the second floor. In two-foot black letters between the second and third floor windows was emblazoned “JUBILEE HOUSE.” To the west of the main build
2 CONC ing stood Jubilee Annex, a two-storey building of similar but less elaborate design with rooms on both levels.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1899, the Jubilee applied for a retail liquor license but was turned down because they hadn’t filled out the application properly. By the end of the year however, the hotel got its license and opened a pub. Reports of pub
2 CONC lic drunkenness became more frequent in The Shoal Lake Star in subsequent years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jubilee’s first operator was Sandy Brown followed by Mrs. Taylor, then Wilson and Coleman who ran an ad in the 1905 Henderson’s Directory offering “new sample rooms, first-class accommodation and the finest liquors and cigars.
2 CONC ” With its handy location near the station, the Jubilee did a booming business, becoming a Shoal Lake landmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jubilee House, its Annex, livery and several other businesses burned to the ground on December 25, 1911 when a stiff northwesterly wind blew the fire down the street, erasing much of the village’s business section. By the end of C
2 CONC hristmas Day, Jubilee House was just ashes and a memory.   
