1 NAME Archibald A /McArthur/
2 GIVN Archibald A
2 SURN McArthur
2 _MARNM McArthur
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 APR 1845
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lobo
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 DEC 1912
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lobo
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Farmer, municipal official.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at Lobo, Middlesex County, Ontario on 24 April 1845, the son of Archibald McArthur and Christina Campbell, he received his education at Komoka Seminary, and began his career as a farmer on leaving school, specializing in stoc
2 CONC k breeding. On 5 December 1872, he married Sarah Ann Gustin.  The marriage was witnessed by James A McArthur of Toronto and Bella McArthur of London.  Archibald and  Sarah had one son, Frederick Joseph Gustin McArthur. He left for
2 CONC  Winnipeg in 1882 and from 1888 to 1891 managed a farm owned by Sir John Kaye at Gull Lake, Assiniboia. He then moved to Winnipeg to found McArthur Grocery Company Limited. He served as a Winnipeg alderman from 1905 to 1908, and w
2 CONC as elected to the Board of Control in 1909, served three terms, and died two days after election for the fourth. McArthur played an active part in bringing Winnipeg’s water supply from Shoal Lake, and in locating a power plant at 
2 CONC Lac du Bonnet.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1911, McArthur was proprietor of the McArthur Grocery Company, 728 Logan Avenue, Winnipeg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 15 December 1912 in Winnipeg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Who’s Who in Western Canada: A Biographical Dictionary of Notable Living Men and Women of Western Canada, Volume 1, 1911. C. W. Parker, editor. Canadian Press Association, Vancouver. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Story of Manitoba
2 CONT
2 CONT by F. H. Schofield The S. J. Clarke Publishing Company, 1913. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pioneers and Early Citizens of Manitoba
2 CONT
2 CONT This collection of biographies of early Manitobans was compiled by the Manitoba Library Association, and published in 1971. Those included in the collection lived prior to 1920, and came from all walks of life: politics, professio
2 CONC ns, business and finance, armed services, arts, pioneers, and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT © 1971, Manitoba Library Association, ISBN 0-919566-01-4 Online version 2007, Manitoba Historical Society. 
