1 NOTE Robert Foster, Harrison Foster, William Williams, and Rawleigh Williams all in the Northwest Indian War 1794
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=8c21ab82-b4cf-413d-97a9-21c5c1f66c89&tid=8627488&pid=-918101742
1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 NOTE HARRISON5 FOSTER (WILLIAM PEYTON4, GEORGE3, WILLIAM2, GEORGE1) was born Abt. 1774 in Prince William Co., VA, and died 27 Jun 1819 in Clark Co., KY. He married ANNA BARTLETT 02 Oct 1799 in Bourbon Co., KY, daughter of WILLIAM BARTL
2 CONC ETT and UNKNOWN. 
2 CONT Notes for HARRISON FOSTER:
2 CONT 1800 Kentucky Taxpayers:
2 CONT FORSTER, Harrison, Bourbon County.
2 CONT Flavius M. Foster, Seedlings of William Foster (Poulsbo, Wash., 1977), p. 29:
2 CONT In 1806, Harrison moved his family to Nicholas County. Here, he became a member of Capt. Gillespie's company of the 13th Regiment of Militia from 1813 to 1816. Here, too, Anna died between 1813 and 1816. Harrison, with his family,
2 CONC  joined his father, William in Clark Co., Kentucky, where Harrison died.
2 CONT
2 CONT NORTHWEST INDIAN WAR: Battle of Fallen Timbers 
2 CONT Source: AMERICAN MILITIA IN THE FRONTIER WARS, 1790-1796, by Murtie June Clark, published by Clearfield.  Robert and Harrison Foster served together in the Battle of Fallen Timbers at the Maumee River, Maumee, Ohio, near present-d
2 CONC ay Toledo.  This battle was a decisive victory for the Americans and resulted in the treaty granting them the right to inhabit the Northwest Territory.  Three of Robert's children married Harrison's children and one of Robert's ch
2 CONC ildren married Harrison's granddaughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harrison Foster pg. 46 - Private - pg. 46  - Major General Charles Scott’s Command, Major Notley Conn ’s Battalion, Capt. Joseph Colvin’s Company: Muster Roll of a Company of Mounted Volunteers, July 10 to Oct 23, 1794 , lost hors
2 CONC e Sept. 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Foster  - Private - pg. 46  - Major General Charles Scott’s Command, Major Notley Conn ’s Battalion, Capt. Joseph Colvin’s Company: Muster Roll of a Company of Mounted Volunteers, July 10 to Oct 23, 1794, lost horse Sept 3
2 CONT
2 CONT NORTHWEST INDIAN WAR: Battle of Fallen Timbers - Aug 20 1794
2 CONT Robert Foster b. 1770,  Harrison Foster b. 1776, and William Williams b. 1775, are found together (below) in Capt. Joseph Colvin's Company of Kentucky Mounted Volunteers. This was part of Major Notley Conn's battalion. Major Conn 
2 CONC was a resident of Bourbon, KY and served in the State Legislature from that county. He was also a presidential elector for Bourbon County and cast his vote for Thomas Jefferson. This is the first record ever found that shows Rober
2 CONC t and Harrison knew each other before the marriages of their children. Robert's son Dennis married Harrison's daughter, Mary Polly; Robert's daughter Ellender married Harrison's son, Harrison Jr., and Robert's son George Archie's 
2 CONC first wife was Phebee, another daughter of Harrison. Robert's son James married another Phebee who was a dranddaughter of Harrison. Robert's brothers-in-law, William and Rawleigh Williams, were also in this war, with William in th
2 CONC e same batallion.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the dates of their service it is concluded that they fought together in The Battle of Fallen Timbers, on August 20, 1794, in Maumee, Ohio, which is near present-day Toledo.  They both lost their horses on the same day.  Gener
2 CONC al Charles Scott commanded the Kentucky Mounted Volunteers in the Wayne Campaign (General "Mad Anthony" Wayne) 1793-94 in the Northwest Indian War. The Battle of Fallen Timbers was a decisive victory for the Americans. A warrior n
2 CONC amed Tecumseh was in this battle, who would later become a chief who would unite five tribes with the Brittish and French against the Americans in the War of 1812 in Ontario, Canada. One of General Wayne's officers was 21-year-old
2 CONC  William Henry Harrison, who would command troops as a General in the War of 1812 and become the 9th President of the United States. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A treaty was finally signed by the Indians and the Americans, in which the Indians agreed to withdraw to northern Ohio, but not all the Indians approved the treaty and there was fighting between Indians and settlers for the next 2
2 CONC 0 years in this region.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1810 US Census, Nicholas County, Kentucky, p. 56:
2 CONT Harrison FOSTER, three males age 0-9, one male age 26-44, two females age 0-9, one female age 26-44.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will Records of Clark County, Kentucky Volume II 28 August 1809-April 1826 Will Records of Clark County, Kentucky Book D OSPage: 444 Name: Harrison Foster  My eldest son, William My eldest daughter, Polley My son, Aaron, My son, H
2 CONC arrison My daughter, Pheby My daughter, Lettis My daughter, Dorcas My brother, Payton Foster, Exe. Witnesses: Thomas Berry and William Foster Written: 21 June 1819 Probated: 20 July 1819 V
2 CONT
2 CONT Clark County (Kentucky) Wills Vol II, Book D, pp. 444-445:
2 CONT In the name of God amen I Harrison FOSTER of the County of Clarke and State of Kentucky Being weak in Boddy but of sound and perfect mind and memory Do make and publish this my Last Will and Testament In manner And form following 
2 CONC That is to say first I give and bequeath To my Eldest son William one sixth part of my Estate after paying My son Aron twenty Dollars which I gave him Extra more Than my other Children. I do also give and bequeath to my Eldest Dau
2 CONC ghter Polley one Cow and one Bell at the appraisement and as much more of my Estate as will make her Equal with her Brother William-I do also give and bequeath to my son Aron one sixth part of my Estate with the addition of twenty
2 CONC  Dollars. I do give and bequeath to my son Harrison one sixth part of my Estate after paying my son Aron twenty Dollars. I do also give and bequeath to my Daughter Pheby one sixth part of my Estate after paying my son Aron twenty 
2 CONC Dollars. I do also give and bequeath to my Daughter Lettis one sixth part of my Estate after paying my son Aron twenty Dollars. I do also give and bequeath to my youngest Daughter Dorcas twenty shillings. I hereby appoint my Broth
2 CONC er Payton FOSTER my sole Executor of this my Last Will and Testament in Witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal the 21st day of June in the year of our Lord 1819. 
2 CONT Signed sealed and Delivered In presence of us
2 CONT Harrison (his mark) Foster (seal) 
2 CONT Thos Berry
2 CONT William Foster
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Court held for Clarke County 20th July 1819 
2 CONT This Last Will and Testament of Harrison FOSTER Deceased was produced before court and proven according to Law by the oath of Thos BERRY and Wm FOSTER Witnesses thereto. Subscribed and ordered to be Recorded and on the motion of P
2 CONC ayton FOSTER the Executor therein named who made oath thereto as the Law directs certificate is granted him for obtaining a probate thereof in due form giving security whereon he together with Wm FOSTER entered into and ackd their
2 CONC  Bond in the penalty of $500 condition according to Law for the due and faithful administrators of this said Decedent estate and performance of his Will.
2 CONT Teste James F. Bullock Clerk
2 CONT Ibid., p. 620:
2 CONT
2 CONT Inventory of Harrison FOSTER, Deceased
2 CONT In obedience to an order from the worshipful Court of Clarke County bearing date July Court 1819 directing us we the under signed to value and appraise the personal Estate of Harrison FOSTER deceased having met on the 29th day of 
2 CONC July 1819 at the house of said dect and after being first sworn before Shelton C. WATKINS one of the acting Justices of the peace for said County do proceed as follows:
2 CONT to wit ($//Cts)
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Harrison Foster was probated July 26, 1819 in Clark co, KY.  His wife had already died so she is not included, but it does name their children: William, Polly, Aaron, Harrison, Phoebe, Lettis, and Dorcas. The administr
2 CONC ator of his estate was his brother Peyton Foster. The Fosters lived in the Indian Old Fields area, near the boundary with Montgomery Co.
2 CONT The kids moved to Switzerland Co, IN, when their parents died; I haven't found who they were staying with there, and then some moved to McLean Co, IL. Most wound up either in Boone Co, MO, or Kansas.
2 CONT Polly was married in Clark co, KY, 1819. William, Aaron, Phoebe, and Harrison Jr. were married in Switzerland Co, IN, 1821-1825. Dorcas was married in McLean co, IL, in 1832, Letticia was married in Boone Co, MO, in 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT Census Data for Harrison Foster 1810 Nicholas Co. Ky.
