1 NOTE "Seedlings of William Foster" by Flavius Foster, Bk II pg. 30.
2 CONT
2 CONT "William, son of Harrison and Anna Foster, grew to manhood in Switzerland County, Indiana, where he met and married Catherine Lineback, and their children were born. In 1828 William's brother, Aaron had moved to McLean, Illinois a
2 CONC nd sent back glowing accounts of the new country. In 1830 William, his brother Harrison Jr. and their families, and their unmarried sister Dorcas migrated to McLean, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT In McLean County William and Harrison Jr. took up land on the Mackinaw River just 1 1/4 miles apart. During the winter of 1830-1831, heavy wet snow caved in the roof of Harrison Jr.'s cabin, and the family nearly froze wading in s
2 CONC now to William's cabin. Here, the two families crowded into the small cabin till Harrison could reroof his house. William died and is buried in McLean Co., Ill. After his death, Catherine married spouse #2, Moses Kuykendal (Kirken
2 CONC dall). and moved to Missouri in about 1841, where she is buried. "
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthplace comes from Family Data Collection, ancestry.com
2 CONT
2 CONT One World Tree shows his birth in Switzerland, Indiana and his death as 8 May 1836 McLean, Ill.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the Black Hawk War of 1830-1832 in Illinois.  He was a private under M. Covell in the Odd Brigade.  Source: Database of Illinois Black Hawk War Veterans
2 CONT http://www.ilsos.gov/GenealogyMWeb/BlackhawkSearchServlet
