1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 NOTE Aaron Foster is in the 1850 census of McLean, Ill, but not with wife Sarah Patton.  His wife in 1850 is Elenor, age 36, birth place not listed.  His birthplace is Kentucky.    Aaron is a merchant with property worth $4000.  All of
2 CONC  his children are listed as being born in Illinois, not Indiana.   The confusion could be because many of them were born in Indiana but others were born in Illinois.  Aaron's second wife, Elenor, may not have not known which child
2 CONC ren were born where.
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/
2 CONT One World Tree says he died in Circleville, Jackson, Kansas.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 Census, Tazewell, Illinois
2 CONT Aaron Foster,  1 male under 5, 1 male 5-10, 1 male 20-30, 1 female under 5, 1 female 10-15, 1 female 15-20
2 CONT Harrison Foster, 2 males under 5, 1 male 20-30, 2 females under 5, 1 female 20-30.  This is Aaron's brother.
2 CONT John Pattan, 1 male under 5, 4 males 5-10, 1 male 10-15, 1 male 15-20, 1 female 10-15, 1 female 15-20, 1 female 20-30, 1 female 40-50.  This is Sarah's brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1840 Census, McLean, IL, includes Aaron, Harrison Foster, several Battertons, Wiley's, Pattons, and a lot of Henlines.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850 Census, Dist. 33, McLean, Illinois
2 CONT Aaron Foster age 46 b. KY, merchant, land value $4000
2 CONT Elenor Foster age 36 b. KY
2 CONT Silas J age 16, laborer, b. Illinois
2 CONT Newton J age 12, b. Illinois
2 CONT Milton S age 6, b. Illinois
2 CONT Samuel C T age 2, b. Illinois
2 CONT Almeda 2 mos. b. Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in Switzerland County, Indiana in 1825 for the marriage of his brother, Harrison.  In 1827 he was In McLean, Illinois. (pg. 55 Seedlings)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Seedlings of William Foster," by Flavius M. Foster, Bk II, Pg. 55
2 CONT "Harrison, 4th child of Harrison & Anna Foster, was born inBourbon co., KY and was taken by his parents to Nicholas and later to Clark County.  Harrison Jr. was barely 12 years old when his last parent, Harrison Sr., died.  In 182
2 CONC 0, Harrison Jr. joined the other children in their hike to Switzerland County, Indina.  Here her grew to manhood, met and married Ellender Foster pg. 74.  Evidently none of her family was in Switzerland County at the time, because
2 CONC  it was Harrison's brother, Aaron who signed themarriage bond.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1830, Harrison Jr., his brother William, their families and unmarried sister Dorcas trekked to McLean Co, IL, joining their brother Aaron who had arrived two years earlier.  With the arrival of Polly in 1842, all of Harrison & 
2 CONC Anna Foster's children had migrated to Illinois, except sisters, Phebee who had died, and Lettice, who lived in Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harrison Jr. and William took up land on the banks of the Mackinaw River opposite the old Patton settlement, about 1 1/2 miles apart.   In putting up his cabin, Harrison chose a faulty ridgepole.  An early snowstorm, wet and heavy
2 CONC , came so deep it was the talked-of event for many years.  Under the weight, the ridgepole gave way during the night, filling the cabin with snow.  Bundling up his family, Harrison Jr. took them 1 1/2 miles upriver to his brother,
2 CONC  William's place.  Here, they remained till Harrison's cabin could be rebuilt.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Adams, who married Aaron Foster's daughter, Margaret, pg. 147, once told how immune Harrison's children had become to the cold.  He said that the children would often skate on the frozen surface of the Mackinaw River barefoo
2 CONC ted.  According to James, Harrison's son Aaron oftern ran around wearing nothing but a long, homespun wool shirt, and barefooted.  Once the three-year-old was missing for quite some time, and when found was fast asleep in a snowdr
2 CONC ift, wearing nothing but his long shirt.  Naturally, the family worried but the little tyke never even caught a cold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tough little rascal, wasn't he?  And I complain when it gets below freezing and warmly dressed.  But, I always did say that ice and snow were for polar bears and penquins!!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Aaron, 3rd child of Harrison & Anna Margaret Foster, was born in Bourbon Co., Kentucky. In 1807, the family moved to Nicholas Co., Ky., and in 1817, to Clark Co., Kentucky. Harrison’s father, William had moved there some years bef
2 CONC ore. It is not known, at this time, when the mother died, but it was between Oct. 22, 1813, when her youngest child was born, and June 20, 1819, when Harrison made his Will.
2 CONT  The children of Harrison and Anna M. Foster were William; Polly; Aaron; Harrison Jr.; Phebee; Lettice and Dorcas. The families of all these were given in earlier chapters, except Gr. Gr. Grandfather Aaron Foster. However, it is n
2 CONC ot known for sure, by me, if William or Polly was the oldest child, nor do I know where the mother was buried. Harrison Foster was buried somewhere in Clark Co., Kentucky.
2 CONT  Around 6 months before Harrison Foster died, Polly had married, and after his death, Lettice was taken by the Davenport family as a ward. It was during their stay in Clark County that a man tried to get two of Aaron’s playmates i
2 CONC nto a fight. Instead of fighting one another, one of the boys picked up a stick and threw it at the man, but his aim was off. The stick hit Aaron in the left eye, putting it out.
2 CONT  In the summer of 1820, William and Aaron took the other children, less Polly and Lettice, through Daniel Boone’s woodlot to Switzerland Co., Indiana. The Foster children had no horses or oxen, so they loaded their belongings onto
2 CONC  the back of some milk cows, and Dorcas was so young, she had to be carried piggy-back much of the way. It must have been a rought trip in some ways, but they had youth and the thought of greener pastures ahead to lure them on. In
2 CONC  Indiana, the Foster children became acquainted with many of the families that were to be their neighbors throughout their lives. Here, too, Aaron, William, Harrison Jr., and Phebee Foster met their mates, and Phebee’s death marre
2 CONC d their happiness.
2 CONT  Aaron Foster became acquainted with the Patton-Wiley family in Switzerland Co., Indiana, and married Sarah Royston Patton. John Patton, her father, also became Aaron’s “father image”, a guiding light, he followed throughout the r
2 CONC emainder of his life. In fact, the children’s memory of their parents, Harrison and Anna M. Foster, grew so dim, Aaron’s son did not even know his grandmother’s name. I had a very difficult time locating her. Aaron and Sarah Patto
2 CONC n were married in 1824, and all was going well with them, when news of rich, unplowed land in Illinois reached them. John Patton was the first to take fire at this news, and passed it on to his son-in-law, who also got a bad case 
2 CONC of foot itch. Sarah was much attached to her lot in Indiana, but reluctantly agreed to go to this new, untamed wilderness, giving up a comfortable home for the doubtful betterment of a frontier land. Her decision was sparked parti
2 CONC ally be the fact that her father, and his family were leading the immigration.
2 CONT  In the fall of 1828, the Patton-Foster convoy hit the road westward. Their lead wagon was pulled by two yoke of oxen, the other two by double teams of horses, and they trailed a herd of cattle and sheep. At first they had little 
2 CONC trouble from the loose stock, but as the miles piled up, and numerous rain-swollen streams had to be crossed, the sheep became more and more reluctant to cross these watercourses, and had to be manhandled over many of them. In lat
2 CONC er years these sheep paid off in meat and wool. In fact, Grandmother Sarah became so proficient with her spinning of the wool, the strands so slender, they were much in demand by her neighbors for sewing thread.
2 CONT  Aaron and John Patton landed in McLean Co., Illinois in November of 1828. While the Pattons lived in the unchanged cabin, described in the heading of the Patton chapter, Aaron Foster was much luckier. An old settler, about the on
2 CONC ly one in that section of Illinois at that time, named John Dawson, gave Aaron a winter’s job. Payment for this work was room and board for Aaron and his family. (Coming into a new land, with so few settlers, how lucky can one get
2 CONC ?) The past winter had been a hard one, on the new arrivals, and Aaron was all for returning to their old Indiana home, but Grandmother Sarah would not hear of such a thing. She had reluctantly given up her old home, on Aaron’s in
2 CONC sistence, and wasn’t just about to return now. Finding that he was out-voted, Aaron told his wife to pick out a plot of land for their new home. She chose a plot adjoining that of her father’s; section 21, Lexington Twp, McLean Co
2 CONC ., Illinois. Later, a second plot was taken up in Section 16. Four hundred acres of land was paid off, mostly by Aaron splitting fence rails at .25 per hundred. The cost of the land, by the government, was $1.26 per acre. Section 
2 CONC 21 was paid off August 17, 1835, and section 16, January 28, 1837.
2 CONT  The first logs for a school were cut in 1830, in Patton Settlement, also known as Old Town Timber. The logs were waiting for a snow so they could be sledded into position, but when the snow did come it lasted so long, and was so 
2 CONC deep the logs could not be moved. In fact, the winter of 1829-30 was long known as “the winter of the big snow”. Then came the big scare of the so-called Black Hawk war, and it was 1832 before the school was ready for its first st
2 CONC udents. The first teacher was one of Sarah’s sisters, and most of the children were from the Foster, Wiley and Patton families. In 1838, Mr. Isaac Smalley, seeking land, arrived on the banks of the Vermillion River, about 40 miles
2 CONC  form Aaron’s claim on the Mackinaw River. The Vermillion was back-full, and while seeking logs for a raft, Mr. Smalley noticed a pile of building material near by. On arriving at the home of Aaron Foster, he was surprised to lear
2 CONC n that he had just passed the future site of Pontiac, Illinois.
2 CONT  Mr. Smalley fell in love with a gently rolling hill on Aaron Foster’s land, and bought 160 acres of the northeast quarter of Section 21. The plot he chose included the old Indian town and burial grounds, mentioned in the heading 
2 CONC of the Patton chapter. In 1840, Mr. Smalley laid out a townsite on this land, expecting a railroad to build through it. Two railroads did build in that direction, but both bypassed the townsite. Mrs. Smalley called a meeting of th
2 CONC e neighbors to find a suitable name for the new town site. Among the gathering was Lydia, wife of Milton Smith, the godfather to one of Aaron’s children. The Milton part of his name still exists today in my middle name. Mrs. Smith
2 CONC  made the following suggestion. “We call our place ‘Poverty Hill.’ And since this is a fairer one to look upon, if I was you, I’d call it ‘Pleasant Hill.’”
2 CONT  A request for a post office hit a minor snag, when it was learned that there was already a Pleasant Hill post office in the state. The post office department suggested that the name be changed to Selma, Illinois, so Pleasant Hill
2 CONC  - Selma, post office was born. The first post master was Harrison Foster Jr., who set aside a portion of his store for the purpose. Methodist Church services had long been held in John Patton’s house, and now a building was built
2 CONC  for the services, with Aaron Foster as class leader.
2 CONT  In 1840, Mr. Absolom Enock arrived with a load of notions, and set up shop in the home of Mr. Smalley. Later, he and Aaron built a building on Aaron’s land, and they went into partnership, a union all that any man could ask for. 
2 CONC He was class leader in his church, a respected merchant with unlimited credit, which he used sparingly, and held some elected positions in Pleasant Hill, at different times. Then disaster struck. Sarah, his wife of many years, sic
2 CONC kened and died. She is buried in the beautiful Pleasant Hill Cemetery, overlooking the bountiful land she helped to conquer.
2 CONT Laurainfrisco@yahoo.com (Laura Frey)
