3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 MAY 1883
2 PLAC Sugar Creek Twp
2 ADDR
3 STAE IA
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source:  Tipton Advertiser, 
2 CONT
2 CONT  May 24, 1883 The sorry news comes that Mr. D.C. Weed, an old and respected citizen of Sugar Creek, is very ill -- so much so that fears are entertained for his recovery. An unfortunate feature of his sickness has been the tempora
2 CONC ry loss of reason.  Since the above was in type we learn of Mr. W.'s death from causes noted.  
2 CONT
2 CONT May 31, 1883 Died Weed-- On the 19th inst., after a short but sever illness, caused by an affection of the brain, Mr. Daniel Corwin Weed, aged 66 years 11 months and 16 days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Weed was born June 3d, 1816, in Orange County, New York, and passed one-half of his earthly life in that county, where he was married to Miss Rebecca Lyon Neafie May 24th, 1843.  Ten year later they removed to Iowa, and in Feb
2 CONC ruary, 1854, they settled on the farm in Cedar County, where they have since lived and where he died  Saturday, May 19th.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Weed was one of our best and most highly respected citizens.  Thrifty, successful and upright in business, he won and maintained the confidence and esteem of the entire community.  He was an exemplary Christian, and a worthy, 
2 CONC honored member of the Congregational church in Wilton from its organization in 1856 to the end of his earthly life.  The church mourns his removal by death as a loss widely and deeply felt.  The entire family--Mrs. Weed, four sons
2 CONC  and one daughter--were all present to tenderly care for, and give Christian burial to, their honored and beloved head.  The funeral services, conducted by the pastor, Rev. E.P. Smith, were held at the family residence Sunday afte
2 CONC rnoon, and were largely attended by an appreciative and warmly sympathetic assembly.
