1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph learned his trade of joiner in Philadelphia, possibly under Charles Plumley, and was in business on his own account by July 1708. He married (i) Rebecca Jennings at Charleston, South Carolina and (ii) Edith 
2 CONC Ward. He is said to have made furniture, coffins, and did other joinery work. Joseph Claypoole is said also to have been one of the more substantial property owners in Philadelphia. He left the mainstream Quakers to join George Ke
2 CONC ith's 'Christian Quakers', and when George Keith was ordained as a minister of the Church of England, Joseph became an Anglican (Episcopalian) and subscribed to the building of the new Christ Church (where notables such as Benjami
2 CONC n Franklin also worshipped). In January 1738, Joseph Claypoole drew up his will leaving his son Josiah all the tools of his trade and all his stock down to "Locks, drops and Scutchins." Although some of these brasses are firmly as
2 CONC sociated with the William and Mary style, he left a maple cabinet and black walnut writing desk to his son James and the largest cedar chest and the "Black walnut Drawers case upon case" to a daughter. If surviving pieces are the 
2 CONC criterion, the last was almost certainly made in what we would call the Queen Anne style, a further indication that the two fashions existed side by side. In May 1738, Joseph Claypoole announced that he had left active work in his
2 CONC  trade and recommended patrons to his son Josiah who was prepared to supply "all Sorts of Furniture of the best Fashion: as Desks of all Sorts, Chest of Drawers of all Sorts, Dining Tables, Chamber Tables, and all Sorts of Tea Tab
2 CONC les and Sideboards." To fashion this furniture, Josiah inherited "the largest and oldest Stock of Timber, of the Produce of this country and the West-Indies of any in this Province, some of which having been in Piles near 25 Years
2 CONC ; he has likewise a Parcel of choice curl'd Maple." 
2 CONT
2 CONT February 15, 1738. Pennsylvania Gazette. To be SOLD, SIX Hundred Acres of Land in Bucks County, scituate on Neshameneh Creek, the South West Branch going thro' the Tract near the Middle: And Four Acres of Pasture Land on Society H
2 CONC ill, fronting Second-Street, fenced with a good Fence of Cedar Posts and Rails. Enquire of Joseph Claypoole in Walnut-Street; who has also to sell sundry Pieces of Silver Plate of several Sorts.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 25, 1738. Pennsylvania Gazette. THIS IS TO CERTIFY. THAT Mr. Joseph Claypoole, Joyner, has left off his Trade; and has given his Stock and Implements of Trade to his Son the Subscriber, who has Removed from his Shop in Walnut-
2 CONC Street, to the Joyners-Arms in Second-Street, near the Proprietor's; where all Persons may be supplied with all Sorts of Furniture of the best Fashion; as Desks of all Sorts, Chests of Drawers of all Sorts, Dining Tables, Chamber 
2 CONC Tables, and all Sorts of Tea-Tables and Sideboards; he having the largest and oldest Stock of Timber, of the Produce of this Country and the West-Indies, of any in this Province, some of which having been in Piles near 25 Years; h
2 CONC e has likewise a Parcel of choice curl'd Maple; and a large Parcel of choice Wood for Bedsteads, and a fine Sortment of the best and newest fashionable Brass Work Furniture, lately Imported from London. JOSIAH CLAYPOOLE
2 CONT
2 CONT October 14, 1742. Pennsylvania Gazette. TO be TAUGHT, in Walnut Street at the House where Mr. JOSEPH CLAYPOLE formerly lived, READING, WRITING, ARITHMETICK, Vulgar and Decimal, MERCHANTS ACCOMPTS, GEOMETRY, ALGEBRA, SURVEYING, TRI
2 CONC GONOMETRY, NAVIGATION, ASTRONOMY, and all other Parts of the MATHEMATICKS, by THEOPHILUS GREW. In a short Time will be published, a Description of GUNTERS SCALE, and its Use in NAVIGATION and ASTRONOMY, by THEOPHILUS GREW, Teacher
2 CONC  of the MATHEMATICKS in PHILADELPHIA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wills: Index to Abstracts, C-E Surnames: 1682 - 1825: Philadelphia Co, PA from PAGenWeb  CLAYPOOLE, JOSEPH. May 3, 1744. G.110. Book G ABSTRACT; CLAYPOOLE, JOSEPH. City of Philadelphia. Joyner. (Written) January 18, 1738/9. (Prove
2 CONC d/probated) May 3, 1744. Children: George, Joseph, Rebecca, Josiah, John, James and Edith. Exec: James and Edith Claypoole. Asst. Exec: John Delwin and Joshua Lawrence. Wit: Benjn. Shoemaker, Samuel Johnson, David Deshler and Amy 
2 CONC Lawrence.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'The Claypoole family of cabinetmakers also emerged as a major player in the cabinetmaking trade. The discovery of a signature, "Jos Claypoole 1743," in chalk under the bottom drawer of the upper case on a mahogany high chest of d
2 CONC rawers, a recent gift to the Philadelphia Museum of Art, is key.  Lindsey attributes the high chest to Joseph Claypoole, who was born in England in 1677 and died in Philadelphia in 1744, (note: this is incorrect, as Joseph was bur
2 CONC ied in 1741)  or to his son Josiah, who was active beginning 1740 (note: actually, 1738 or earlier). The case piece not only identifies the Claypoole shop but its distinctive ogee pediment, related to Dutch/German/English architec
2 CONC ture, which is a signature of this early Philadelphia style. This distinctive pediment appears on James Logan's desk-and-bookcase with mirrored doors and on a secretary desk of the finest Honduran mahogany, loaned to the exhibitio
2 CONC n by Tyron Palace Historic Sites and Gardens in South Carolina.'  Source; 'The Claypoole Family Joiners of Philadelphia: Their Legacy and the Context of Their Work' Andrew Brunk,  2002 Chipstone American Furniture Journal, 
2 CONT
2 CONT "42. This chest of drawers has been attributed to either Philadelphia "Joyner" Joseph Claypoole (1677-1744) who advertised that he had "left off his Trade" by 1738 or his son Josiah Claypoole who by 1740 had left Philadelphia to a
2 CONC dvertise in South Carolina "all sorts of Joyner's and Cabinet-Maker's Work, as Desk and Book Cases, with Arch'd. Pediment and O.G. Heads...." Prime, Arts & Crafts, pp. 162-163, transcribing Joseph Claypoole advertisement, Pennsylv
2 CONC ania Gazette, May 18, 1738, and Josiah Claypoole advertisement, The South Carolina Gazette, March 22, 1740; remarks of Andrew Brunk, "Arts of Baroque Pennsylvania," November 12, 1999; Worldly Goods checklist #39". Source: Bulletin
2 CONC  of the American Philosophical Society, Winter 2001
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Joseph was the youngest son of James Claypoole (1634-1687) and Helena Mercer Claypoole (ca 1638-1688), Quakers, who arrived on the 'Concord' at Philadelphia on October 1, 1683. Orphaned at the age of 11 years and 1 month, Joseph w
2 CONC as apprenticed as a joiner, starting the Claypoole furniture/cabinetmaking business that continued through his sons, George (1706-ca 1770), Josiah (1718-1756) and grandson, George (1733-1793). Joseph was an active member of the co
2 CONC ngregation of Christ Church and is said to have become one of the wealthiest men in Philadelphia. He married Rebecca Jennings on July 17, 1703 at "Charles towne" (Charleston), "South Carrolinah". Rebecca died on 30 November 1715, 
2 CONC leaving Joseph with an 11 day old son, Josiah, (1715-1716) and five other children aged 11, 8, 6, 4 and 19 months. Four months later, on April 1, 1716, he married Edith Ward at Christ Church, Philadelphia. Together, they had 5 chi
2 CONC ldren of whom two died in childhood/infancy. 
