1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born:   1608 [Torrey]. 1608 - died 1684, aged 76 [Perley]. 1618 - He was called aged forty-eight years in 1666. [Austin, John Osborne. One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Massachusetts. 1893.]
2 CONC   Immigrated:   Possibly by 1637 - per Savage. He would have been 29.  Spouse:   Unknown, per Torrey. He gives no dates, so I know no other particulars of her life. Torrey indicates first born child was 1641, suggesting they marri
2 CONC ed perhaps in 1639-40, and also suggesting that she was an immigrant, herself (as a maid or accompanying a family), or a "lost" daughter of another immigrant. Hopefully, the continuing "wave" of identification of wives over the la
2 CONC st 20 years from English research will continue and turn up her identity and perhaps link us to yet another immigrant family, that of her father.  Residence:  Salem, Essex Co, Mass, apparently his whole stay in the New World (Torr
2 CONC ey, Savage). Although indications are that his being Quaker did cause him to move around a bit, to avoid persecutions.  Savage on John:  "JOHN, Salem, a tanner, from 1637 to 1684, when he’d have been worried for a Quaker, 1659, 60
2 CONC  and 1.  See Felt. "  The History of Salem by Sidney Perley, Vol. 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg 384: "Capt. John-1 Burton was a planter and tanner; died in the automn of 1684; children: 1.  John-2, born about 1641; living in 1684; 2.  Samuel; living in 1684; 3.  Hannah, born about 164-; married William Osburn; 4.  Isaac."
2 CONC   Pg 117: "Signers of the Oath of Allegiance to the King, required by the General Court  order of 2 Oct 1678, included William OSBURNE, John BURTON, sr., and John  BURTON jr., among others." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants of Lawrence and Cassandra Southwick of Salem, Massachusetts, Pg 75 
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1658, Josiah Southwick, John Small, and John Burton were arrested for being Quakers, at Dedham, Mass., while on their way to Rhode Island to provide homes for themselves and families and to escape from the intolerant pers
2 CONC ecutions of the Puritans. They were released and resumed their journey.--Felt's Annals of Salem, Vol. 2, p. 581. Colonial America, 1607-1789 MA Census Index, Pg 7 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1661 census, Salem, Essex Co, Mass. Pg 151 Austin, John Osborne. One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Massachusetts. 1893. [of] Salem, Mass.  He was a tanner.  1637.  He was at Salem at this early date, hav
2 CONC ing come thence via Barbados.  His sympathies were strong for the Quakers, and he early joined that sect.  1658, 3.  He and Josiah Southwick were apprehended at Dedham for being Quakers, they being then on their way to Rhode Islan
2 CONC d to provide a residence for themselves and families, and to escape from their persecutors; they were released and resumed their journey, but did not stay long in Rhode Island, returning to Salem within a year or two.  1660, 1, 27
2 CONC .  He and others of Salem were prosecuted, at Salem, for religious beliefs; and he and his son Isaac and daughter Hannah were several times arrested for non-attendance on public worship.  1666.  He was then called aged forty-eight
2 CONC  years.  
2 CONT
2 CONT References included above are:  Savage > Dictionary of New England Settlers  Torrey > New England Marriages Before 1700  Perley > History of Salem  Austin, John Osborne, One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts. 1893. estral" type published genealogy. Also any NEHGR or TAG articles.  The little material I have on him thus far has come from older works, including primarily:    Savage > Dictionary of New England Settlers  Torre
2 CONC y > New England Marriages Before 1700  Perley > History of Salem John Burton NOT the son of Boniface  There are reports, probably originating from fanciful 19th century genealogies that show Boniface Burton as father of John. Boni
2 CONC face Burton had no children (none recorded, none mentioned in his will, yet he mentioned a niece, his wife had a will and she likewise  referred to no children, yet she referred to folks who may have had a kinship, but not childre
2 CONC n: Anderson, Sanborn, and Sanborn, The Great Migration (... continues), NEHGS, Vol 1, pp 504-506, 1999. This is the most authoritative treatment of Boniface Burton we have at the moment.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Immigrant 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born:   1608 [Torrey]. 1608 - died 1684, aged 76 [Perley]. 1618 - He was called aged forty-eight years in 1666. [Austin, John Osborne. One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Massachusetts. 1893.] Immigrated:   P
2 CONC ossibly by 1637 - per Savage. He would have been 29.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:   Unknown, per Torrey. He gives no dates, so I know no other particulars of her life. Torrey indicates first born child was 1641, suggesting they married perhaps in 1639-40, and also suggesting that she was an immigrant, h
2 CONC erself (as a maid or accompanying a family), or a "lost" daughter of another immigrant. Hopefully, the continuing "wave" of identification of wives over the last 20 years from English research will continue and turn up her identit
2 CONC y and perhaps link us to yet another immigrant family, that of her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Residence:  Salem, Essex Co, Mass, apparently his whole stay in the New World (Torrey, Savage). Although indications are that his being Quaker did cause him to move around a bit, to avoid persecutions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:   1684, presumably in Salem (Torrey, Perley), aged 76.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will / Estate: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "1684, 10, 14.  Will, proved 1684, 11, 16. Executor, son John. Witnesses, Robert Fuller, Richard Croade, Sr. He mentions sons John, Samuel, and Isaac, son-in-law William Osborne, and William Osborne's wife Hannah, also mentions gr
2 CONC andchild Samuel Osborne and other grandchildren. For overseers of the will he appointed his friend Robert Fuller, and son-in-law William Osborne. 1684, 11, 16.  Inventory, œ223, 12s. taken by Samuel Aborne Sr. and Isaac Cooke. On 
2 CONC the same date, depositions of Henry Kenney, aged 60 years, and John Nichols, aged 44, were given as follows: "That John Burton, Senr. deceased, did several times in way of discourse s'd that he in case his 2 sons John and Samuel s
2 CONC hould die, not having any heirs of their bodies lawfully begotten, that then his son Isaac and the heirs of his body should inherit his land; because he would have it continue in the name of Burton."
2 CONT
2 CONT From the will of his son Isaac2 it is seen that neither of his brothers left issue; and Isaac2 thus heiring the land, left to his son Isaac3, the reversion in same property. There was henceforward no trouble, probably, from lack o
2 CONC f heirs, as Isaac3 (Isaac,2 John1) had two wives and thirteen children."
2 CONT
2 CONT Will citations from: Austin, John Osborne. One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Massachusetts. 1893.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.genealogieonline.nl/en/watson-gladstone-family-tree/P398.php
