2 CONC ostly hay and kept from40 to 50 head of stock. He was also interested in wool growing with his brother, William B., and they owned over 3000 head of sheep. He was also a stockholder in the Central Utah Wool Co., the Messenger Prin
2 CONC ting Co., president of the North Six-Mile Creek Irrigation Co., and secretary and treasurer of the Pioneer Water Co. He was a policeman for about fourteen years. City Marshal a number of years and Constable. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He built a nice residence in town. Mr. Lowry was one of the substantial men of Manti and an honor-able and upright citizen. He married March 13, 1870, Heneretta, daughter of William and Henrietta Anderson, who was also born in Man
