2 DATE 29 OCT 1899
2 ADDR
3 CITY Manti
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} OBITUARY: The Manti Messenger, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Saturday 28 October 1899:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Temple City Mourns The Sad Loss of A Faithful and True Woman, Mrs. Ethel Lowry Reid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Monday, a telegram reached this city announcing the fact that Mrs. Ethel Lowry Reid, beloved wife of Clare W. Reid, and daughter of John Lowry, Sen., and wife, arrived in Provo very sick. The telegram was received before the 
2 CONC northbound train left this city and John Lowry and family and Bishop W. T. Reid and family immediately prepared to leave for Provo that day which they accomplished and found their daughter in a most critical condition. The sad new
2 CONC s of her death reached this city by telegram Wednesday last and the sorrowful news spread over the city in a very short time, and a gloom and a melancholy feeling was noticed upon the countenance of evry person made familiar with 
2 CONC the sad affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT A little over a year ago deceased together with her husband left Manti for the Samoan Islands to fulfill a mission and there was no thought in the minds of any that she would be brought home in her coffin. The remains were brought
2 CONC  to Manti today and the respect shown deceased by the young ladies and gentlemen at the depot in whose company she was formerly an associate and highly respected, acted as pall-bearers and carried the remains to her home where she
2 CONC  will remain until tomorrow, when the funeral services will be held in the Tabernacle at 2 o'clock.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Reid were married in this city about four years ago and lived happily together during the time she was permitted to enjoy the breath of life, and the sad ending of their happiness is most deplorable to the young husba
2 CONC nd who was left in Samoa to fulfill his mission at the request of his deceased wife before she left for Utah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT We are told that it was his desire to accompany her but she thought it best he perform his duty by remaining, truly showing that she was a true and faithful Latter day Saint in works, actions and every particular.
2 CONT
2 CONT A child was born about six months ago to Mr. and Mrs. Reid while in Samoa, and this together with other troubles they were obliged to contend with was no doubt the cause of her illness and death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased was born in this city July 6, 1871, and four years ago this month she was married in the Salt Lake Temple to the faithful laborer in the cause of truth now in Samoa. The sad news was cabled him as far as the cable runs, a
2 CONC nd no one can comprehend the feeling he will have when the heart rending and cruel news reaches his field of labor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral tomorrow will be one of the largest ever held in this city as the deceased was a faithful worker among the young people, holding the position of president of the Y. L. Association until shortly before she was called to
2 CONC  labor in the missionary field.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that faithful young people like these, away from home and sent on an errand by the Almighty to promulgate his Gospel to the inhabitants of the earth should experience such a trial is beyond comprehension, and the only con
2 CONC solation the young husband can get is that his beloved wife was a faithful and true worker in the cause they left this city to teach, and that God in His infinite wisdom thought it best to call this daughter of His to the other wo
2 CONC rld where a greater work will be awaiting her.
2 CONT
2 CONT May the bereaved husband and family be comforted is our desire for them, and let us all pray to Almighty God to comfort and give the young husband, (who will not be here to attend the funeral) strength to bear the sad intelligence
2 CONC  with enough ease that will keep him from all evil that might be surrounding him in this world of trials.
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains may be viewed at the residence of John Lowry, Sr., from noon to 1:30 p.m. on Sunday.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manti Messenger, Saturday 4 November 1899: Funeral Services.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services over the remains of Mrs. Ethel Lowry Reid were held in the Tabernacle last Sunday and the large building gave the appearance of a conference, so large was the attendance. Apostle George Teasdale was present and wa
2 CONC s the first speaker. He gave the bereaved family words of sympathy and said the Quorum of Apostles had offered up a prayer to Almighty God in behalf of the bereaved husband who was fulfilling his mission in Samoa, that when the sa
2 CONC d news reached him his sorrow would be lessened and that he would realize that it was the will of the Lord that his wife was called to the other world to continue her mission. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elders Edward Wood, N. G. Stringham, and Leroy Clayton were the missionaries who accompanied Mrs. Reid to Utah from the Islands and they all testified that deceased was a faithful worker in the cause she was called to labor and ev
2 CONC er willing to do all she was required to do. Elder Wood stated that when deceased was sitting on the ground before a class of natives teaching them, they would often get behind her to get a chance to touch her clothing, showing th
2 CONC at the natives truly had confidence in her teachings and dearly loved her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other speakers were Presidents Canute Petersen and John B. Maiben who spoke a short time in praise of the deceased and President Henry Beal pronounced the benediction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twelve young men of this city acting as pall bearers carried the remains to the cemetery. They were Spencer Moffitt, Bruce Cox, H. E. Coolidge, Horace Brown, James Wodskow, Nephi Bessey, Russell Moffitt, Bernard Parry, Louis Houga
2 CONC ard, Jr., J. A. Hougaard, Herbert Smyth and P. A. Poulsen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were over fifty vehicles following and hundreds of citizens walking to the cemetery. Beautiful flowers were sent from different parts of the State by relatives and friends which added greatly to the beautiful casket the dear
2 CONC ly beloved sister was placed in.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the cemetery President John D. T. McAllister dedicated the grave and the remains was laid at rest surrounded by masonry. The funeral was one of the saddest ever witnessed in the Temple City. G. E. Anderson, brother-in-law of Cl
2 CONC are W. Reid, by permission photographed the interior of the Tabernacle for the benefit of the bereaved husband in Samoa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lowry (1829 - 1915)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Jane Brown Lowry (1834 - 1920)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Clare W Reid (1872 - 1958)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart Lowry Reid (1899 - 1916)
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dutiful, brave and faithful her life a sacrifice to her motherhood and her God.
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Vauna Mri
2 CONT
2 CONT Record added: Jan 28, 2009
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave Memorial# 33358677
2 CONT --------------------
