2 CONC husband who was fulfilling his mission in Samoa, that when the sad news reached him his sorrow would be lessened and that he would realize that it was the will of the Lord that his wife was called to the other world to continue he
2 CONC r mission. Elders Edward Wood, N. G. Stringham, and Leroy Clayton were the missionaries who accompanied Mrs. Reid to Utah from the Islands and they all testified that deceased was a faithful worker in the cause she was called to l
2 CONC abor and ever willing to do all she was required to do. Elder Wood stated that when deceased was sitting on the ground before a class of natives teaching them, they would often get behind her to get a chance to touch her clothing,
2 CONC  showing that the natives truly had confidence in her teachings and dearly loved her. Other speakers were Presidents Canute Petersen and John B. Maiben who spoke a short time in praise of the deceased and President Henry Beal pron
2 CONC ounced the benediction. Twelve young men of this city acting as pall bearers carried the remains to the cemetery. They were Spencer Moffitt, Bruce Cox, H. E. Coolidge, Horace Brown, James Wodskow, Nephi Bessey, Russell Moffitt, Be
2 CONC rnard Parry, Louis Hougaard, Jr., J. A. Hougaard, Herbert Smyth and P. A. Poulsen. There were over fifty vehicles following and hundreds of citizens walking to the cemetery. Beautiful flowers were sent from different parts of the 
2 CONC State by relatives and friends which added greatly to the beautiful casket the dearly beloved sister was placed in. At the cemetery President John D. T. McAllister dedicated the grave and the remains was laid at rest surrounded by
2 CONC  masonry. The funeral was one of the saddest ever witnessed in the Temple City. G. E. Anderson, brother-in-law of Clare W. Reid, by permission photographed the interior of the Tabernacle for the benefit of the bereaved husband in 
2 CONC Samoa.
