1 NOTE birthplace: Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Bull, eldest child, and only son, of Isaac Bull and his 1st wife,Mary, was called a miller in North Kingston, Rhode Island. He and hiswife Mary Closson had ten children, of whom one, Ruth, may have diedyoung as no further re
2 CONC cord of her has been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT Six of their sons, (all excepting John) moved into New York Colony aboutthe same time, some settling in Dutchess County. Mary's brother, NathanClosson, had migrated to Dutchess County so this may have influenced thesix young Bull 
2 CONC brothers to "go west" and eventually settle there. Theyhad large families which later helped to colonize the wilderness of otherparts of New York in the north and west. Some went to Vermont, thesouthern part of which was New York 
2 CONC at that time, During and following
2 CONT the Revolutionary War, some of their descendants went up into Quebec andsome eventually migrated westerly across Canada." Descendents of JosiahBull Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Bull and Mary Closson are:
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Isaac Bull, born July-22-1708 in Jamestown, R.I.; died Abt 1777 inKent, Ct; married Rebecca Browning.
2 CONT ii. John Bull, born June-08-1710 in S. Kingston, R.I.; married LydiaClosson.
2 CONT iii. Hannah Bull, born 1712; married Eben Harrington.
2 CONT iv. Josiah Bull, born 1714 in Kingston, Rhode Island; died in DutchessCounty, NY; married Ruth Tripp 1737 in S. Kingston, R.I..
2 CONT v. Nathan Bull, born 1715 in S. Kingston, R.I.; died 1791 in Rutland, VT;married Abigail Inman.
2 CONT vi. Ruth Bull, born 1717 in S. Kingston, R.I..
2 CONT vii. Timothy Bull, born 1720 in S. Kingston, R.I.; married Patience Page.
2 CONT viii. Jeremiah Bull, born 1722 in S. Kingston, R.I.; married (1) AbigailMan(n); married (2) Ruth Closson.
2 CONT ix. Jacob Bull, born 1725 in S. Kingston, R.I.; married Rebecca Gamage.
2 CONT x. Abigail Bull, born 1727; married Abraham Palmer.
