1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was a farmer residing at Charlestown, RI. He describes his farm and mentions his four daughters and wife. He gives to the heirs of his son Caleb, five silver dollars; if they came for it within one year of his d
2 CONC eath. His son John was sole Executor and had the Estate after debts and bequests to daughters and widow were made. After debts were paid and terms of the will executed the farm was sold on March 30, 1793. Ichabod's Homestead was s
2 CONC till known as the Closson Farm in 1916.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: The spelling of "Closson" often became "Clawson" at this time. Probably because of phonetic spelling of census takers.
2 CONT
