1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT from the Atlantic Daily Telegraph, Thursday, February 23, 1911
2 CONT DEATH OF MRS. REBECCA MOTT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT Had Been Ill Ten Days
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT Elderly Lady Mother of a Number of Prominent People Dies at Her Daughter's Home
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT Mrs. Rebecca Mott died last night at the home of her daughter, Mrs. G. J. Pellett after a serious illness of about ten days.  Death was due to complications caused by old age.
2 CONT Mrs. Mott, whose maiden name was Rebecca Brown was born in England, Sept. 7, 1824 being at the time of her death 86 years and 5 months and 15 days of age.  Her mother died when she was four years old.  At the age of nine years she
2 CONC  with her father left England and went to Canada to live. She was married in 1847 to R. C. Mott.  In 1869 they removed with their family to Wisconson where they resided thirty-three years then coming to Cass county where her husba
2 CONC nd died in the year 1899.  They were the parents of nine children, seven of whom are still living.  The children are: George Mott, Seymour, Wis., Joseph Mott, Canada, Mrs. Jno. Wood, Pestigo, Wis., deceased; Mrs. Geo. Pellett, Atl
2 CONC antic, Ia., Mrs. Frank Herbert, Atlantic, Mrs. W. R. Mick, Carroll, Neb., Annie Mott, deceased; Mrs. O. W. Peterson, Atlantic, Mrs. (sic Mr.) H. A. Mott, Atlantic.
2 CONT All of the children were born before Mr. and Mrs. Mott left Canada.
2 CONT Deceased has been a member of the Methodist church from childhood days.
2 CONT The funeral will be held Saturday afternoon at 1:30 from the Methodist church.  Interment will be in the Atlantic cemetery.  Some of the grandchildren will act as pallbearers.
2 CONT {errors in the above obituary:  1) the last child was Mr. H. A. Mott (not Mrs.), 2) date of birth was Sept. 17, not Sept. 7}
2 CONT
2 CONT from unknown newspaper source
2 CONT Mrs. Rebecca Mott, born Sept. 17, 1824.  Died Feb. 22, 1911
2 CONT Mrs. Rebecca Mott passed away last evening at the home of her daughter, Mrs. George Pellett, at 9:50 o'clock after an illness of a little more than a week, tho; her health has not been the best for some time.  Old age and its comp
2 CONC lications and an attack of the grippe were the causes of death.
2 CONT Rebecca Brown was born in Devonshire, England and came to Canada when nine years of age.  She was married in February, 1846, to Richard C. Mott. In 1869 they came to the United States and until 1878 lived in Manitowoc, Wisconsin. 
2 CONC  They then removed to Iowa to farm in Brighton township.  She lived there until the death of her husband in November 13, 1899.  Rebecca then lived with her son H.A. Mott until 1905 when she went to live with her daughter, Mrs. Osc
2 CONC ar Peterson.
2 CONT Since last June she has resided with her son-in-law and daughter, Mr. and Mrs. George Pellett of 10th and Chestnut Streets.  Nine children were born to the Motts, seven of whom are now living.  They are Mrs. Pellett and Mrs. Frank
2 CONC  Herbert, of this city, Mrs. O.W. Peterson, south of Atlanta, and Joseph Mott of Brockville, Ontario.  Mrs. Lucy Wood and Mrs. Anna Wortman preceded their mother in Death.  Besides the children, 39 grandchildren and 31 great-grand
2 CONC children survive her.
2 CONT Funeral services will be held at 1:30 o'clock, Saturday afternoon, from the M.E. Church conducted by Rev. Griffith and interment will be in the Atlantic cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT ***her obituary indicates her mother must have died in England***
2 CONT ***Rebecca's tombstone indicates she died in 1910, the above obituary was in the 1911 newspaper***
