1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT from unknown newspaper source:
2 CONT IDA L. MICK
2 CONT Ida L. Mick was born in the Dominion of Canada, June 14, 1861.  She lived there with her parents until she was eight years old when they moved to Wisconson, U. S. A., and later moved to Atlantic, Iowa.  Here she grew to womanhood 
2 CONC and married W. R. Mick, January 1, 1883.
2 CONT To this union were born five children.  They are Mrs. Lou Surber, Wayne, Nebr.; Mrs. Clark Mossman, Leith, N. D.; Mrs. Donald Porter, Wayne, Nebr.; Mr. Otto Mick, Washington, Kansas; Mrs. Ed. Honey, Holton, Kansas.
2 CONT These children, and ten grandchildren survive her.  She also leaves her husband, W. R. Mick of Washington, Kansas, and three brothers and two sisters.
2 CONT Mrs. Mick united with the Methodist Episcopal church in Carroll, Nebraska, about twenty-five years ago, moving from there to the Methodist church at Neosho, Mo., and later transferring her membership to the Methodist church, Holto
2 CONC n, Kansas.  She became a member of the Golden Rule Sunday school class in the Methodist church, Holton, and was highly regarded there while serving with the class, and always as a friend and comrade.
2 CONT She died Tuesday, January 26, 1926, at the home of her daughter, Mrs. Ed. Honey, in Holton, Kansas, where she had been for some weeks having come there from her home in Washington, Kansas, that she might have the care of her daugh
2 CONC ter in this time of suffering.  Her acute illness, however was of only a few days duration, and she slipped away quietly to rest.
2 CONT "There is a land where beauty cannot fade,
2 CONT Nor sorrow dim the eye;
2 CONT Where true love shall not droop, nor be dismayed,
2 CONT And none shall ever die."
2 CONT The funeral services were held in the Methodist church at 2 o'clock, Friday afternoon, January 29, by the pastor, and interment was made in the Holton cemetery, Holton, Kansas
2 CONT R. O. Penick, Pastor
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT CARD OF THANKS
2 CONT We wish to thank our friends and neighbors for the kindness shown us during the illness and death of our beloved wife and mother.
2 CONT W. R. MICK AND CHILDREN
2 CONT
2 CONT from the Holton Recorder, January, 1926
2 CONT MICK
2 CONT The death of Mrs. W. R. Mick of Washington, Kans., occurred at the home of her daughter, Mrs. E. L. Honey, in Holton Tuesday night at 11:30.  She had been ill at her daughter's home since the latter part of December, suffering wit
2 CONC h the malignant growth.  Mr. Mick came Sunday and other relatives expected for the funeral are a son, O. R. Mick of Washington, Kans.; and daughters, Mrs. Lou Surber of Wayne, Nebr., Mrs. Matie Porter of Wayne, Nebr., and Mrs. Eth
2 CONC el Mossman of Leith, N. D.  The funeral services will be Friday and the burial in Holton cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT from death certificate 43 2236, State of Kansas
2 CONT Ida L. Mick, born June 14, 1861 in Toronto, Canada to Richard Mott who was born in Canada and Rebecca (no last name given) who was born in England, cause of death was carcinoma of liver
2 CONT
