1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''George Vail Gould'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/208964026/george-vail-gould
2 CONT
2 CONT Antecedents—Son of George Gould (b. Litchfield, Litchfield County, Connecticut, September 2, 1807, d. Troy, Rensselaer County,  New York, December 6, 1868) who was graduated at Yale College in 1827, and was a distinguished chief j
2 CONC ustice of the New York Supreme Court of Appeals, and of Sarah McCoun Gould (Vail). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Justice George Gould was a son of Connecticut chief justice James Gould (b. Branford, Connecticut, 1770) whose grandfather, William Gould (b. North Tawton, Devon, England, 1693) emigrated in 1720, and took up his residence at Bran
2 CONC ford, Connecticut. The wife of Chief Justice James Gould was Sally McCurdy Tracy, the daughter of Major General Uriah Tracy, for ten years U.S. Senator from Connecticut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. George Gould was the daughter of George Vail of Troy, President of the New York State Agricultural Cociety and the first importer of Durham cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life—Born in Troy, N.Y., October 24, 1851, studied at Williston Seminary, East Hampton, Massachusetts, and was with the Class during freshman and sophomore years and one term of junior year. Member of Delta Kappa, Phi Theta 
2 CONC Psi and Psi Upsilon.
2 CONT
2 CONT After career—Leaving New Haven on account of his health, he joined Williams College '73, with which he was graduated, receiving at the same time an honorary degree of A.B. from Yale College. He read law with C. L. Tracy of Troy, a
2 CONC nd was graduated at the Albany Law School in 1875. He practiced for some years in Troy, and later had an office in what was then the suburb of Lansingburgh, but toward the close of his life did not practice much. He died at his mo
2 CONC ther's house in Lansingburgh, New York on March 24, 1887, of pneumonia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death he was a justice of the peace, a Knight Templar, and a thirty-second degree mason and was prominent among the young republicans of Lansingburgh. It was said of him that he had one of the brightest legal mi
2 CONC nds in his part of the state. He never married.
2 CONT
2 CONT —Yale College Catalog 1901, p. 209
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will, dated August 26, 1885, George Vail Gould of the village of Lansingburgh, Rensselaer County, New York, provides for his funeral expenses, and names his brother, Edward Tracy Gould of Brooklyn, Kings County, New York, a
2 CONC nd his mother, Sarah McCoun Gould (Vail) of Lansingburgh, among others.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://lansingburghhistoricalsocietyarchives.org/the-village/village-officials/trustees-of-the-village-of-lansingburgh/ Trustee of the Village of Lansingburgh], 1877
