0 @N6000000003949526187@ NOTE  Jonathan Weeks, Sr. with oldest sons, Jonathan and Philip, came to Wyoming in 1762 from New Fairfield, Fairfield Co., Conn., where he had purchased one full share of the Susquehanna Land Co. Escaping the massacre of 1763 with all
1 CONC  his family, he returned to Wyoming in 1771,his two sons, Philip and Thomas having returned in 1769 to occupy their land a second time. In 1776 Jonathan and Abigail were members in good standing of the Baptist Church of Westmorela
1 CONC nd Co. Speaking of the Wyoming Massacre, July 3, 1778, Peter Fritz, History Northampton Co., Pa. page 17 says: 'From the farm of Mr. Weeks seven went out to battle, five sons and sons-in-law and two inmates. All seven perished.' O
1 CONC n the Wyoming Monument the names of six are in the list of those killed (including Josiah Carman, inmate) but the name of the seventh, ----Gates (probably Robert Gates, inmate) is in the list of those who survived the battle,   Ru
1 CONC pp's History Northumberland Co.,Pa., page 319, says: 'Anthony Turkey, and Indian formerly at Wyoming, was with the Indians during the Massacre. After the battle his party came to the house of Mr. Weeks the Sunday following and tak
1 CONC ing the old gentleman's hat, shoved his rocking chair into the street and sat down and rocked himself.'  "Another family upon whom the blow fell with great force and severity was that of Mr. Jonathan Weeks. He resided upon a large
1 CONC  farm, with his sons and son-in-law, about a mile below the borough of Wilkesbarre. He had living with him at the time of the alarm, his three sons, Philip, Jonathan and Bartholomew. Silas Benedict, a son-in-law (Miner, in his His
1 CONC tory of Wyoming, page 238, gives an account of the massacre in which he says Silas Benedict married a granddaughter of Jonathan Weeks
1 CONT Page 238 , Chas Miner "History of Wyoming" 1845.  Story quoted as follows:   From the farm of an aged man by the name of Weeks, in Wilkes Barre, originally in Fairfield Co, seven persons had gone out to battle, (so imperious, so i
1 CONC rresistible were demands for men, even to make up the three hundred.).   Philip, Jonathan and Bartholomew Weeks, his sons - Silas Benedict, who married a granddaughter, Jabez Beer, and Josiah Carman, relatives, and Robert Bates, a
1 CONC  boarder.  Horrible slaughter:  The whole seven lay dead on the field at night.  A band of Indians came to the house of Mr Weeks and bade him remove.  "How can I?" said he, "my whole family you have slain."  Getting provisions, th
1 CONC ey feasted heartily, when one of them wheeled a large rocking chair into the road, took the hat from the old gentleman, and putting it on his own head, sat down and rocked himself.  Allowing him to take a pair of oxen, they gave M
1 CONC r Weeks three days to prepare for his departure, when they set fire to the buildings, and destroyed all that was left. 
1 CONT Weeks Family, Jonathan Weeks of TITL TYPE:Book PERI: Weeks Family, Jonathan Weeks of New Fairfield, CT andWyomingValley, PA, AUTH Raymond Weeks
0 @N6000000005077151760@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT The deed which establishes his relationship to Philip is found in Vol III pp 515-516 Oyster Bay Town Records. A deed for land bought at Round Hill, in Horse Neck, Greenwich, Connecticut, is found in land records Vol III ; 338 Town
1 CONC  Hall Greenwich, Connecticut, dated 5 December 1728. A deed to land at New Fairfield is found in Fairfield Town Records Vol 4 ; 141, dated 5 April 1736. Early in 1762  Jonathan contracted for one share of the Susquehanna Company w
1 CONC hich had purchased land of the Indians in Wyoming Valley, where Wilkes-Barre now stands. (Hamblin)
0 @N6000000001212409255@ NOTE Notes 2005:  From the genealogy of the Poppino Family
1 CONT (http://www.popenoe.com/Poppino%20Family/i0000005.htm) the following information was found:
1 CONT Jonas Poppino was born in Orange Co., New York before 1765.  Jonas died after 1830.  He married Eleanor Thompson.  Eleanor died after 1830.   Eleanor was the daughter of Captain George Thompson and Elizabeth Jayne.  The 1810 Censu
1 CONC s shows Jonas in Warwick, Orange Co., New York.  The 1820 and 1830 Census shows him in Blenheim, Schoharie Co., New York.  One history says "Isaac Moffatt Jr. married Marry Poppino".  Mary was the daughter of Jonas and Eleanor Pop
1 CONC pino, pioneers who settled on the farm later owned by Charles Mayham.  This area is at the north end of the Catskills on the border f Delaware and Schoharie Counties.
1 CONT _____
