0 @N6000000000953521049@ NOTE
1 CONT Jared Newman Worden (son of Stephen) came to Iowa about 1856 with his family from Dutchess County, New York.  His sons were mostly grown at the time.  Joseph and Husted were in the Civil War.  There was Silas, (Laura Worden-Avery’
1 CONC s-grandfather) Edward, Charley, Emmeline, Phoebe, Amanda, Mary Cornelius, and Rosella (Ella).  All of these children had families, except Phoebe.  Silas Worden and two sons came to McPherson County, Nebraska,  before the turn of t
1 CONC he century, taking homesteads and there after making that county their home except that Silas returned to Iowa later. Also Ella Worden-Priest’s family came to that county as well as other Worden relitives to make their homes.  It 
1 CONC was a pioneer period for sure.
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------   
1 CONT History of Jared Newman Worden:  
1 CONT Jared was named for a neighbor of his father's, Jared Newman.  Jared Newman Worden married Mary Tacy Denton in 1833.   They had eleven children, ten of whom were born near the town of Pawling, Dutchess County, New York.  The last 
1 CONC child was born in Decatur County, Iowa.
1 CONT From an account written by Meda Worden Langsdorf, as told by her father Joseph Watkins Worden, about the move from New York to Iowa:  How the family of Jared Newman WORDEN came to Iowa.  Jared's son Joseph was 21 years old in 1855
1 CONC .  That spring, Joseph traveled to Iowa in the company of Lyman Chase and family, of Holmes, Dutchess County, New York.  They settled northwest of Garden Grove, Iowa, a settlement founded a dozen years earlier by the Mormons.  Jos
1 CONC eph was so delighted by the country, he returned early the next sping to New York to try and persuade his father to sell his 30 acre farm near Holmes in Dutchess County and move his family to Iowa.  Joseph saw the possibilities th
1 CONC ere were for them (10 children in the family, 6 of them were boys and 4 girls).  The mother's health was very poor.  Joseph thought that the climate might be beneficial to her health.  Joseph thought that with so much wonderful ch
1 CONC eap land, the 6  boys would have a great chance to improve their lot.  In later years, 5 of them owned farms all joining.  Husted owned a farm in Nebraska.
1 CONT The father was undecided at first about moving.  He had lumber and rock on his place and a cellar dug for a new house, as the old one was of log.  Finally he said that he would sell for $1800.00.  Joseph and Silas got very busy fi
1 CONC nding a buyer. A man by the name of Joe Holmes offered $1500.00 but Jared wouldn't go down in price.  In order to make the deal, Joseph and Silas borrowed $200.00 from a cousin, Silas Abbott, giving the money to Holmes to make the
1 CONC  $1800.00.  Although Jared did not find out for a long time about his sons' "deal", he was more than pleased with the beauties of the far west.  Jared sold his household goods, ox team, cows, chickens, and implements.
1 CONT In about a month the family was ready to leave for the "Golden West".  The morning they were to leave, the relatives and many neighbors all gathered, giving gifts and prayers.  Many tears were shed at parting, thinking they would 
1 CONC never see them again and that the Indians would kill them.  Joseph, their first son, writes of their leaving  New York state; "In the spring of 1856 they gathered all their earthly possessions together and left the little rocky fa
1 CONC rm on the side of the mountains, and with all their children started for the far west.  Jared and his wife, with their 10 children, 7 large trunks and many other packages, such as their wagon tent and wagon cover, left Pawling, Ne
1 CONC w York by train.  They then took a boat across to Canada, then a train to Chicago.  Silas, who had the family dog to look after, became confused in Chicago when two trains left almost simultaneously, one going East and one going W
1 CONC est.  His mother soon missed him and nearly went frantic.  Finally he was located and the train brought him back.  The mother had quite a job taking along food for this large family on the train.  They got off the train at Fort Ma
1 CONC dison, Iowa and from there took a boat up to Burlington, from there they bought tickets for the only train in Iowa, which took them about 15 miles out on the open prairies.  There was not one thing in sight except a little store b
1 CONC uilding that kept a few supplies.  There they unloaded their baggage and Jared and the boys got busy.  Pitched their tent, and then struck out to buy an oxen team and wagon which they did find.  They then put on the wagon cover wh
1 CONC ich they had made in New York and brought along on the journey.  After loading their trunks and small children, they started for Garden Grove.  All who could walk  did.  It took four days to get there.  From there they went to Lym
1 CONC on Chase family, northwest of Garden Grove,  there was a small log cabin near there where the family moved to until fall when their own house was bulit and made ready for the family. 
1 CONT When They settled on the beautiful prairies in Decatur County, Iowa,  Jared and his wife thought it almost a paradise.  When not otherwise occupied, the farmer would travel over the beautiful natural meadows.  But he was not long 
1 CONC to enjoy the scenes in which he so much delighted to spend his idle hours.  The summer of 1859 was a very wet year.  As it drew to a close, there were many cases of chills and fever.  He took congestive chills, passing away in a s
1 CONC hort time on his Iowa farm, at the age of 51, after only 3 years out west.  The mother did not survive long afterwords.  She had been an invalid for a number of years.  Jared was a life long farmer.  Jared is buried in Metier Ceme
1 CONC tery, Garden Grove, Decatur County , Iowa.
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Countied, Iowa, (Lewis Publishing Company (1887). pp. 562-63  
1 CONT
