1 CONT Jared Newman Worden 
1 CONT
1 CONT WORDEN
1 CONT
1 CONT Posted By: William Baker <scotsman_2001@yahoo.com>
1 CONT Date: 2/11/2002 at 01:32:05 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Biography of Jared Newman Worden 
1 CONT
1 CONT HISTORY OF JARED NEWMAN WORDEN -- Our ancestor Jared Worden was born on May 25, 1808 between Holmes and Pawling, Ducthess County, New York on his parent's farm. He died on September 29, 1859 of a stroke on his farm near Garden Gro
1 CONC ve, Decatur County, Iowa. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared was named for a neighbor of his father's in Duchess County, New York, Jared Newman. Jared Newman Worden married Mary Tacy Denton in 1833. They had eleven children, ten of whom were born near the town of Pawling, Duchess Coun
1 CONC ty, New York. The last child was born in Decatur County, Iowa. 
1 CONT
1 CONT From an account written by Meda Worden Langsdorf, as told by her father Joseph Watkins Worden, about the move from New York to Iowa: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "How the family of Jared Newman Worden came to Iowa. Jared's son Joseph was 21 years old in 1855. That spring, Joseph traveled to Iowa in the company of Lyman Chase and family of Holmes, Dutchess County, New York. They settled nor
1 CONC thwest of Garden Grove, Iowa, a settlement founded a dozen years earlier by the Mormons. Joseph was so delighted by the country, he returned early the next spring to New York to try and persuade his father to sell his 30-acre farm
1 CONC  near Holmes in Dutchess County and move his family to Iowa. Joseph saw the possibilities there were for them--10 children in the family, 6 of them were boys and 4 girls. The mother's health was very poor. Joseph thought that the 
1 CONC climate might be beneficial to her health. Joseph thought that with so much wonderful cheap land, the 6 boys would have a great chance to improve their lot. In later years, five of them owned farms all joining. Husted owned a farm
1 CONC  in Nebraska. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "The father was undecided at first about moving. He had lumber and rock on his place and a cellar dug for a new house, as the old one was of log. Finally he said that he would sell for $1800.00. Joseph and Silas got very busy find
1 CONC ing a buyer. A man by the name of Joe Holmes offered $1500.00, but Jared wouldn't go down in price. In order to make the deal, Joseph and Silas borrowed $200.00 from a cousin, Silas Abbott, giving the money to Holmes to make the $
1 CONC 1800.00. Although Jared did not find out for a long time about his sons' "deal", he was more than pleased with the beauties of the far west. Jared sold his household goods, ox team, cows, chickens and implements. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "In about a month the family was ready to leave for the Golden West. The morning they were to leave, the relatives and many neighbors all gathered, giving gifts and prayers. Many tears were shed at parting, thinking they would nev
1 CONC er see them again and that the Indians would kill them. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Joseph, their first son, writes of their leaving New York state: 'In the spring of 1856 they gathered all their earthly possessions together and left the little rocky farm on the side of the mountains, and with all their children
1 CONC  started for the far west. Jared and his wife, with their 10 children, 7 large trunks and many other packages, such as their wagon tent and wagon cover, left Pawling, New York by train. They then took a boat across to Canada, then
1 CONC  a train to Chicago. Silas, who had the family dog to look after, became confused in Chicago when two trains left almost simultaneously, one going East and one going West! His mother soon missed him and nearly went frantic. Finall
1 CONC y he was located and the train brought him back. The mother had quite a job taking along food for this large family on the train! They got off the train at Fort Madison, Iowa and there took a boat up to Burlington, From there they
1 CONC  bought tickets for the only train in Iowa, which took them about 15 miles out on the open prairies. There was not one thing in sight except a little store building that kept a few supplies. There they unloaded their baggage and J
1 CONC ared and the boys got busy, pitched their tent, and then struck out to buy an oxen team and wagon which they did find. They then out on the wagon cover which they had made in New York and brought along on the journey. After loadin
1 CONC g their trunks and small children, they started for Garden Grove. All who could walk did. It took four days to get there. From there they went to the Lymon Chase family, northwest of Garden Grove. There was a small log cabin near 
1 CONC there where the family moved to until fall when their own house was built and made ready for the family.' [The Jared Worden house was later owned by his son Silas Worden. A picture of Jared's son Joseph Watkins Worden's house was 
1 CONC in the book, "History of Decatur County, Iowa", I have a photostatic likeness. I do not believe that Joseph's house still stands, it did until the 1950's at least.] 
1 CONT
1 CONT "When they settled on the beautiful prairies in Decatur County, Iowa, Jared and his wife thought it almost a paradise. When not otherwise occupied, the farmer would travel over the beautiful, natural meadows. But he was not long t
1 CONC o enjoy the scenes in which he so much delighted to spend his idle hours. The summer of 1859 was a very wet year. As it drew to a close, there were many cases of chills and fever. He took congestive chills, passing away in a short
1 CONC  time on his Iowa farm, at the age of 51, after only 3 years out west. The mother did not survive long afterwards. She had been an invalid for a number of years." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared was a life long farmer. Jared is buried in Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur County, Iowa. His tombstone states that he was 51 years, 1 month and 1day old when he died, however this was in error. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from the memoirs of Jared's son, Joseph Watkins Worden, as preserved by his daughter, Meda Worden Langsdorf (1875-1976). Written in April 23, 1901: "I will write a few lines of my life since I left the eastern hil
1 CONC ls for the (then) far west. I left the 12th day of May, 1855. I was just past 21 years of age. This part of the west was then really a new country- - -very thinly settled. Was some government land and land was very cheap, some sel
1 CONC ling as low as one dollar per acre. The first summer I was here I saw the first wild Indians- - a part of the Potawatamie Tribe- - 40 in number. They were peaceful, disturbing no one. Deer and wild turkey were plentiful. Two or th
1 CONC ree days later I was in Nebraska before the government land was surveyed- - the land where Lincoln is a large city. Land in that part of the state is now worth from one to three hundred dollars per acre. At that time there were a 
1 CONC good many Buffalo ranging where now are good sized cities. There was not a mile of railroad in this state of Iowa when I came here- - now there are more miles than any other state except Illinois. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "The 27th day of May, 1860, I married a worthy young woman, Cinthia M. Davis. We had a farm of 200 acres to put to cultivation, fence, break the prairie sod, build a house and other improvements. She took suddenly sick and wanted 
1 CONC to go back to her old home in Pennsylvania. Took her back. She seemed to improve for a few days, but on the fourth of July she passed away. I went back home to assist my mother in taking care of her large family. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "In the winter of 1861 and 1862 Mother, my brother Husted, and I talked over the conditions of the government. It was certain the boys were needed at the front. Mother said she hated to see us go, but we owed a duty to our country
1 CONC . We put in the crop the following spring and on the 26th of July 1862, Brother Husted and I enlisted in Co. B 18 Iowa Infantry and started for the front. When I bid my mother good bye I was certain it was the last time in this wo
1 CONC rld." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared Worden's wife, Mary Tacy Denton Worden died 1863, July 12, while her two of her older sons were in the Union Army. After Mary died, the task of caring for the younger children fell upon the next older son, Silas. 
1 CONT
1 CONT A LITTLE ABOUT JARED NEWMAN WORDEN’S NEIGHBOR, LYMAN CHASE -- According to the memoirs of Joseph Worden, the reason that the Wordens came to Iowa was Lyman Chase. Lyman Chase was a neighbor of Jared’s in Duchess County, New York, 
1 CONC near Pawling (and Holmes). After Lyman went west to Decatur County, Iowa, word went back to Lyman’s former neighbors about the new lands suitable for farming, just for the claiming, in Iowa. The Newmans were also neighbors of Jare
1 CONC d’s in New York and representatives of that family were in Decatur County, Iowa as well. Jared was named after his father’s neighbor in Duchess County, Jared Newman. The Chases were featured in a biography in the "History of Decat
1 CONC ur County" (Chase biography, p. 662). A brother of Lyman, Hiram Chase also came to Decatur County. Hiram and Lyman and their wives are buried in Garden Grove Cemetery, just north of the town of Garden Grove. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The history of Doze Cemetery in Garden Grove Township, Decatur County, Iowa mentions an unmarked grave with a single burial in a pasture, Ruth A. Newman, the daughter of Lyman B. and Elizabeth (Mead) Chase whose farm home was loca
1 CONC ted in Section 17 where this grave was located. Ruth became gravely ill. Evidently sensing her numbered days, she said as she looked out a window... "If I die bury me over there under that oak tree in the green pasture." she died 
1 CONC in her 19th year, and left a husband and one son. About 1955, this land was bulldozed and the tombstone was found and placed along a fence. Today the marker cannot be located. The Newmans were neighbors of the Wordens back in Dutc
1 CONC hess County, New York. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The 1930’s era WPA Cemetery records show a Cemetery in Section 17, but no one has ever heard of a Doze Cemetery. (Victor Doze was elected a judge of Garden Grove Township in July 1850 and may have owned this land at one time.) The
1 CONC  name of this woman buried here has also been called "Osborn". WPA Records state: Ruth Chase Newman d. 186? AE 23. Doze Cemetery on the Charles Roe farm, Garden Grove Township. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared had purchased a 140 acre homestead in Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa. After the death of Jared and his wife, their son Silas bought out the interests of the rest of the heirs in the homestead. He later added 560 acr
1 CONC es to the place and eventually had 700 improved acres of farmstead. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared, as well as his sons, were Republicans in political affiliation. The book, "Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Counties, 1887, has the biographies of his sons Joseph W., Edward T. and Silas, as well, 
1 CONC as Lyman Chase. Other books with biographies of Jared's children are: "McPherson County, Nebraska: Facts, Families, Fiction" by Laura Worden Avery, et al.. and the book, "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahern, page 266, Decatur C
1 CONC ounty, Iowa library. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: 
1 CONT 1. An account by Meda Worden Langsdorf, based on the memoirs of her father Joseph W. Worden. 
1 CONT 2. The book, "Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Counties, 1887, 796 p. 
1 CONT 3. Tombstone of Lyman and Hiram Chase, Garden Grove Cemetery, Garden Grove, Iowa. 
1 CONT 4. "McPherson County, Nebraska: Facts, Families, Fiction" by Laura Worden Avery, et al..* 
1 CONT 5. "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahern, Decatur County, Iowa library. 
1 CONT 6. 1810 Pawling, Dutchess County, New York Census. 
1 CONT 7. 1820 Pawling, Dutchess County, New York Census. 
1 CONT 8. 1830 Pawling, Dutchess County, New York Census. 
1 CONT 9. 1840 Pawling, Dutchess County, New York Census. 
1 CONT 10. 1850 Pawling, Dutchess County, New York Census. 
1 CONT 11. Descendants of Jared Newman Worden. 
1 CONT 12. Family File of William R. Baker.
