0 @N6000000006414563168@ NOTE Will / Estate:
1 CONT
1 CONT "1684, 10, 14.  Will, proved 1684, 11, 16. Executor, son John. Witnesses, Robert Fuller, Richard Croade, Sr. He mentions sons John, Samuel, and Isaac, son-in-law William Osborne, and William Osborne's wife Hannah, also mentions gr
1 CONC andchild Samuel Osborne and other grandchildren. For overseers of the will he appointed his friend Robert Fuller, and son-in-law William Osborne.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1684, 11, 16.  Inventory, œ223, 12s. taken by Samuel Aborne Sr. and Isaac Cooke. On the same date, depositions of Henry Kenney, aged 60 years, and John Nichols, aged 44, were given as follows: "That John Burton, Senr. deceased, di
1 CONC d several times in way of discourse s'd that he in case his 2 sons John and Samuel should die, not having any heirs of their bodies lawfully begotten, that then his son Isaac and the heirs of his body should inherit his land; beca
1 CONC use he would have it continue in the name of Burton."
1 CONT
1 CONT From the will of his son Isaac2 it is seen that neither of his brothers left issue; and Isaac2 thus heiring the land, left to his son Isaac3, the reversion in same property. There was henceforward no trouble, probably, from lack o
1 CONC f heirs, as Isaac3 (Isaac,2 John1) had two wives and thirteen children."
1 CONT
1 CONT Will citations from: Austin, John Osborne. One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, Privately Printed. Massachusetts. 1893.
0 @N6000000003006476180@ NOTE [hapton.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT !BOOK: Town of Tioga, page 462. The next advent of settlers appears tohave
1 CONT been Lodowyck Light, Jesse Miller, the elder, and Enos Canfield, who camefrom
1 CONT the vicinity of Bedford, Westchester county, and located upon a tract offive
1 CONT hundred acres, known as the "Light & Miller location," next west of twhere
1 CONT Samuel Ransom located, extending southwesterly well toward the village of
1 CONT Smithboro. They came on, made clearings and built their houses in aboutthe
1 CONT year 1787, and brought on their families about the year 1790.  LodowyckLight
1 CONT first built a small cabin, upon a small knoll nearly directly across theroad
1 CONT from the residence of the late Henry G. Light., and soon afterwards builthis
1 CONT log house newar the site of the latter, near the old well thereon, wherehe
1 CONT lived until his death. August 26, 1830, being a month and four days past
1 CONT seventy-eight years of age.  His wife, Martha, died September 28, 1842,four
1 CONT months and four days past eighty-six years of age.  The remains of these
1 CONT venerable pioneers lie buried in the old burying-ground, upon the top ofthe
1 CONT hill, about one mile east of Smithboro village.  This old burying-groundis now
1 CONT in a sorry state of dilapidation, and its condition ought to be improvedat
1 CONT once for the sake of its preservation.  This is also the site of the first
1 CONT meeting house in the town of Tioga, built in 1812, by the Methodist andBaptist
1 CONT denominations.  It was destroyed by lightning in the summer of 1826, andthere
1 CONT are those who are so irreveretn as to say that, it was the meet judgmentof the
1 CONT wrath of God, because of the intensity of the denominational jealousiedand
1 CONT unchristian differences between the two church organizations.  LodowyckLight
1 CONT was a prominent citizen, and took an active part in the politcal affairsof his
1 CONT day.
