1 CONT Jared WORDEN's wife, Mary Tacy Denton-Worden died 1863, July 12th, while two  of her older sons were in the Union Army.  After Mary died, the task of caring for the younger children fell upon the next older son, Silas.
1 CONT Mary is buried in Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur, Iowa.  Some know her by Mary Tracy Denton-Worden.
0 @N6000000000953524272@ NOTE
1 CONT Joseph joined Whaley Baptist Church in Dutchess County, New york, as a young man.  Moved from New York to Iowa on May 12, 1855.  Joseph took homesteaded land in 1865 and lived there until his death.  Joseph enlisted with his broth
1 CONC er Husted in Co. B. Iowa Infantry on July 26,1862.  Joseph was a Civil War veteran.  The following is from the memoirs of Joseph Watkins Worden, as preserved by his daughter, Meda Worden-Langsdorf.  Written in April 23, 1901.   " 
1 CONC I will write a few lines of my life since I left the eastern hills for the (then) far west.  I left the 12th day of May, 1855.  I was just past 21 years of age.  This part of the west was then really a new country, very thinly set
1 CONC tled.  Was some goverment land and land was very cheap, some selling as low as one dollar per acre. The first summer I was here I saw the first wild Indians- a part of the Potawatamie Tribe--40 in number.  They were peaceful, dist
1 CONC urbing no one.  Deer and wild turkey were plentiful.  Two or three days later I was in Nebraska before the goverment land was surveyed-- the land where Lincoln is a large city.  Land in that part of the state is now worth from one
1 CONC  to three hundred dollars per acre.  At that time there were a good many buffalo roaming where now there are good sized cities.  There was not a mile of railroad in this state of Iowa when I came here--now there are more miles the
1 CONC n any other state, except Illinois. 
1 CONT "The 27th day of May, 1860, I married a worthy young woman, Cinthia M. Davis.  We had a farm of 200 acres to put to cultivation, fence, break the prairie sod, build  a house and other improvments.  She took suddenly sick and wante
1 CONC d to go back to her old home in Pennsylvania.  I took her back.  She seemed to improve for a few days, but on the fourth of July she passed away. I went back home to assist my mother in taking care of her large family.
1 CONT "In the winter of 1861 and 1862 Mother, my brother Husted and I talked over the conditions of the goverment.  It was certain the boys were needed at the front.  Mother said she hated to see us go, but we owed a duty to our country
1 CONC .  We put in the crop the following spring and on the 26th of July 1862, brother Husted and I enlisted in Co. B 18 Iowa Infantry and started for the front.  When I bid my mother good-bye I was certain it was the last time in this 
1 CONC world."
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Joseph Worden later married Priscilla Delk, fathered six children, and lived out his remaining years on his homesteaded land in Decatur County, Iowa.  He was a farmer.  Another source is the book, "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale
1 CONC  Ahern, Pg. 266.
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Joseph is buried in Row 8, Stone 5, Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur, Iowa.
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Countied, Iowa, (Lewis Publishing Company - 1887), pp.562-63:
1 CONT
1 CONT “J.W.Worden, a native of Dutchess County, New York, ws born April 10, 1834, a son of Jared N. and Maria T. (Denton) Worden.  He remained at home until his majority, and in the spring of 1855 strted, in company with L.B. Chase, for
1 CONC  Iowa, Garden Grove being their objective point.  They came with the intention of making a permanent settlement if they found the country to be in accordance with the estimate others had put upon it.  Being pleased with the outloo
1 CONC k, he remained a year, and in the meantime bought eighty acres of land.  In 1856 he returned to his native state, and in the spring of 1856 started again for the West, and accompanied by his father and family.  His father bought 1
1 CONC 40 acres of land in Franklin Township, which is now a part of the farm owned by his son Silas.  Mr. Worden Subsequently sold his first purchase and bought the farm which is now his home, on section 15, Franklin Township.  This he 
1 CONC has brought under cultivation, and has erected good building improvements.  He owns 180 acres of the best land in the township, and his farm shows the result of having a thrifty and energetic owner.  July 26, 1862, Mr. Worden enli
1 CONC sted in the war of the Rebellion, and was assigned to Company B, Eighteenth Iowa Infantry, and served his country three years, returning home uninjured, although he hed many narrow escaped, and saw much hard service. He was marrie
1 CONC d in May, 1860, to Miss Cynthia H. Davis, daughter of William Davis.  She died the July following her marriage.  November 5, 1865, he married Miss Priscilla L. Delk, daughter of John and Sarah Ann Delk.  They have had a family of 
1 CONC six children - J. Frank, Harry Colfax, Emma Kate, Ida A., Meda M. and Ira A.  Harry Colfax is deceased.”
1 CONT
1 CONT HISTORY OF JOSEPH WATKINS WORDEN 
1 CONT By William Baker 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph was named after Joseph Wadkins, a next door neighbor to his grandfather Steven Worden, and no doubt also a friend to his father Jared. Joseph joined Whaley Baptist Church in Duchess County, New York in 1853. He moved from N
1 CONC ew York to Decatur County, Iowa on May 12, 1855. Joseph homesteaded land there in 1865 and lived there until his death in 1912. Joseph enlisted with his brother Husted in Co. B Iowa Infantry July 26, 1862. Joseph was a Civil War V
1 CONC eteran. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from the memoirs of Joseph Watkins Worden, as preserved by his daughter, Almeda Worden Langsdorf. Written in April 23, 1901: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "I will write a few lines of my life since I left the eastern hills for the (then) far west. I left the 12th day of May, 1855. I was just past 21 years of age. This part of the west was then really a new country- - -very thinly se
1 CONC ttled. Was some government land and land was very cheap, some selling as low as one dollar per acre. The first summer I was here I saw the first wild indians- - a part of the Pottawamie Tribe- - 40 in number. They were peaceful, d
1 CONC isturbing no one. Deer and wild turkey were plentiful. Two or three days later I was in Nebraska before the government land was surveyed- - the land where Lincoln is a large city. Land in that part of the state is now worth from o
1 CONC ne to three hundred dollars per acre. At that time there were a good many Buffalo ranging where now are good sized cities. There was not a mile of railroad in this state of Iowa when I came here- - now there are more miles than an
1 CONC y other state except Illinois. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "The 27th day of May, 1860, I married a worthy young woman, Cinthia M. Davis. We had a farm of 200 acres to put to cultivation, fence, break the prairie sod, build a house and other improvements. She took suddenly sick and wanted 
1 CONC to go back to her old home in Pennsylvania. Took her back. She seemed to improve for a few days, but on the fourth of July she passed away. I went back home to assist my mother in taking care of her large family. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "In the winter of 1861 and 1862 Mother, my brother Husted, and I talked over the conditions of the government. It was certain the boys were needed at the front. Mother said she hated to see us go, but we owed a duty to our country
1 CONC . We put in the crop the following spring and on the 26th of July 1862, Brother Husted and I enlisted in Co. B, 18th Iowa Infantry and started for the front. When I bid my mother good bye I was certain it was the last time in this
1 CONC  world. She died 1863, July 12." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph Worden later married Priscilla Delk, fathered 6 children, and lived out his remaining years on his homesteaded land in Decatur County, Iowa. He was a farmer. Another source is the book, "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahe
1 CONC rn, page 266, as well as the book, "The History of Weldon, Iowa", where his house is depicted. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph is buried in Row 8, Stone 5, Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur County, Iowa. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1910 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census Joseph is listed with a Loyd Hensley, grandson, age 16, born in Iowa, in Joseph's household. Joseph's son Ira, age 37 single, is listed in the 1910 Census. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1880 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census Joseph is listed with a Fernando Delk, laborer and a S. Abbot, 24, boarder, book agent in his family's household. 
1 CONT
1 CONT OBITUARY OF JOSEPH WATKINS WORDEN: 
1 CONT "Joseph W. Worden, son of Jared and Maria Worden, was born in Duchess County, New York, April 10, 1834, and died November 1, 1912, aged 78 years, 6 months and 21 days. With his parents he came to Iowa in the year, 1856. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He and Priscilla L. Delk were united in marriage on November 05, 1865, and they lived happily together until death came and called Mrs. Worden to the better world on September 18, 1908, since that time Mr. Worden continued to live
1 CONC  in his old home until death came suddenly on November 1, 1912 and called him to a better state of existence where he and his wife are reunited. Mr. Worden was the father of six children, namely: John Franklin, Harry Colfax, Emma 
1 CONC Kate, Ida Elmira, Almeda Marie, Ira Albert. Harry Colfax died November 19, 1869. Ida Elmira died September 13, 1900. Mr. Worden united with the Baptist Church in early life at the age of 18 years, and his life since that time has 
1 CONC been a very consistent one, being practical in his religious life and work, in his daily life showing the spirit of his master Jesus. His loyalty to his country is shown in the fact that he enlisted in Co. B, 18th regiment, Iowa V
1 CONC olunteer Infantry, July 26, 1862 and was dischraged July 20, 1865, serving three years and six days. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Worden was the eldest of six brothers. Two of the six brothers have passed over the river. Mr. Worden, in his life and example, has had much to do with the advancement of this community. He was an ideal citizen, a kind neighbo
1 CONC r, a man loyal to his country and the church, a Christian gentleman, a kind and indulgent father. 
1 CONT
1 CONT A brief funeral service was held in the home where he has lived for 45 years November 3, at 1 p.m. conducted by the Rev. George E. Mitchell. The singing was by a quartette from Weldon. the interment was at the Metiere Cemetery. Th
1 CONC e pall bearers were composed of Comrades of Worden's fellow veterans. The bereaved have the sympathy of the entire community." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes about the Baptist Church that Joseph joined, Whaley Baptist Church. Taken from the book, "HISTORY OF DUTCHESS COUNTY, NEW YORK", By James H. Smith, 1882, Chapter XLVII, Part Four, THE CHURCHES OF PAWLING: -- "In 1853, a new 
1 CONC and comfortable church edifice was built near the south end of Whaley Pond. The First Baptist Church is still prosperous, and is performing a good mission in that part of the town. The pulpit is supplied now by Rev. William B. HAR
1 CONC RIS, of the Luddingtonville church. The Clerk of the society is Van Ness DENTON." (Whaley Baptist Church) 
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death, Joseph's 180 acre farm in Section 15, Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa was taken over by his son Ira Worden. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: 
1 CONT 1. "The History of Weldon, Iowa", page 112-113. 
1 CONT 2. "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahern, page 266. 
1 CONT 3. 1900 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census. 
1 CONT 4. 1910 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census. 
1 CONT 5. Cemetery Records of Decatur County, Iowa, at the Leon Library, Leon, Iowa. 
1 CONT 6. 1901 Memoirs of Joseph W. Worden, related by Almeda Worden Langsdorf (1969). 
1 CONT 7. The History of Dutchess County, New York", By James H. Smith, 1882, Chapter XLVII, part four.
0 @N6000000000953519966@ NOTE
1 CONT Silas was named after his paternal uncle.  Silas was born in New York.  He came with his family in 1856 to Deactur County Iowa.  Silas farmed in Morgan Township, Decatur County, Iowa, section 8, listed in 1875 Platte book.  Silas 
1 CONC was a farmer, Silas moved to McPherson County, Nebraska about 1897.
1 CONT U.S.census-12 Jul 1870 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa-page 4, line 32: Worden, Silas, age 34- occupation:farmer-born:New York
1 CONT U.S.census-29 June 1880, Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa-page 1[188].line 9 Worden, Silas, age:43-occupation:farmer-born:New York-Father:New York-Mother:New York.
1 CONT
1 CONT On May 19th, 2002 we (Ron & Betty Marlin, Terrie Hengen, Lila Brooks and Gloria Marlin) walked the Green Bay Cemetery, (aprox. 10 miles south, 1mile west, 1/2 mile north of Osceola, Clarke County, Iowa).  We found a headstone that
1 CONC  has the following engraved on it;
1 CONT Silas Worden
1 CONT Born Sep 19, 1837
1 CONT Died Oct 23, 1911
1 CONT Also had a GAR Civil War Veteran star beside his headstone.  His daughter Almina F. Worden is in the plot to the south of him.  They are located in the middle section toward the back of the cemetery.
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Dectur Counties, Iowa, (Lewis Publishing Company - 1887), p 648
1 CONT
1 CONT “Silas Worden is a native of New York State, born in Dutchess County, a son of Jared N. and Maria T. (Denton) Worden, natives also of the Empire State.  In 1857 he accompanied his parents to Decatur County, Iowa, and made his home
1 CONC  with them until manhood.  His father bought 140 acres of land in Franklin Township, where he made his home until his death.  After the death of his parents Silas bought the interests of the rest of the heirs in the homestead, whi
1 CONC ch he now owns, and to which he has added 560 acres, having now a fine farm of 700 acres, all under cultivation.  His residence is a model of convenience and comfort, and his farm buildings are large and substantial.  Mr. Worden w
1 CONC as married when twenty-seven years of age, to Laura A., daughter of Abraham and Sarah (Dibble) Roach.  They have a family of five children - Luther, Walter, Perry, Frances Elmina and Abbey,  In politics Mr. Worden affiliates with 
1 CONC the Republican party.
0 @N6000000000953524285@ NOTE
1 CONT He was part of the advance group of his family to come out by train to Iowa, before his father.  His father didn't want to sell their farm in New York, unless he got the right price.  The older sons of Jared Newman WORDEN conspire
1 CONC d to borrow & raise half of the money from relatives & gave it to an Uncle.  The uncle then bought their dad's place & they then could move to Iowa.  It was only much later that Jared found out about it.  On July 26,1862, Warren H
1 CONC usted WORDEN enlisted in the Union army.  After the war he settled in McPherson county, Nebraska.  He was always referred to as Husted.  Warren was named after his maternal uncle.  Warren Husted DENTON.
0 @N6000000000953524317@ NOTE
1 CONT Worden, Charley Jared, D. 17 Jan 1913 Franklin township, Decatur County., IA  age- 61 years 11 months 15 days; white male; Born - 2 Feb. 1851 in New York; Married; Occ: farmer; Burial 19 Jan1913, Johnson Cemetery, Decatur County, 
1 CONC Iowa funeral director; H.O. Tuttle, Van Wert;certifier O.P. Jamison 22Jan 1913; Cause of death; Lobar Pneumionia; father: Jared N. Worden B. NY; Mother Maria T. Denton; Informant: Mrs. Chas Worden, Weldon IA." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Biography of Charles Jared Worden, 
1 CONT By William Baker 
1 CONT
1 CONT HISTORY OF CHARLES JARED WORDEN -- Our ancestor Charles was born on February 02, 1851 in on his father's farm, situated between Pawling and Holmes, Dutchess County, New York and died on January 17, 1913 in Weldon, Franklin Townshi
1 CONC p, Decatur County, Iowa. He was the ancestor of many descendants, some still living in the county, others in Nebraska and elsewhere. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the spring of 1856, when Charles was 5 years old, he moved out to Iowa with his family. On March 16, 1877 he married Anna Eliza Piburn at the farm home of her father, William R. Piburn, in Harrison County, Missouri. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles and his family moved to Lincoln County, Kansas by the next year, 1878. He moved back to Iowa shortly after 1885 (death of infant son David) and before 1888 (birth of son Elbert). He spent the rest of his life in Iowa near 
1 CONC Weldon. He farmed all his life. Eventually, Charles and Anna took care of his father-in-law when Mr. Piburn became aged. Charles was about five foot seven inches tall, with black hair. He was a very handsome man and in his old age
1 CONC  sported a long full flowing beard. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Obituary of Charles Jared Worden: 
1 CONT "Charles J. Worden, son of Jared N. and Maria T. Worden, was born in Dutchess County, New York, February 2nd, 1851, where he lived until 5 years of age. In the spring of 1856, his parents came to Iowa and settled on a farm 2 miles
1 CONC  south of Weldon, later occupied by Silas Worden. Charles was one of eleven children, six of whom are still living. They are Husted, Edward, Cornelius, Amanda Manchester, Mary Trisler, Rosella Priest. The dead are Joseph, Silas, E
1 CONC maline, Daniel and Phoebe. 
1 CONT
1 CONT On March 19th, 1897, Mr. Worden was married to Anna Eliza Piburn. To them were born eight children, who are Hattie, Lissie, Arthur, Numan, David, Elbert, Lottie and Harvey. David died August 18th, 1885, while the parents were stil
1 CONC l living in Kansas. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Worden began their wedded life about 3 mile southwest of Weldon in a log cabin that stood near where W. A. Machlan now lives, the second year they moved to Kansas where they remained until the year 1888 when they retu
1 CONC rned to Iowa, and moved onto a farm four miles south of Weldon which has since then been their home. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles J. Worden died January 17, 1913 at the age of 61 years, 11 months and 15 days. His death was due to a weak heart and an attack of pneumonia that lasted only a few days. Thus the third one of the Worden brothers has departe
1 CONC d this life in the past 15 months. Funeral services were held at the home Sunday, January 19, by the Reverend George Mitchell, of Weldon, and the body laid to rest in Johnson Cemetery, two miles east of the Fairview Church." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles is buried in Row 6, Stone 2 (or row 2) in Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur County, 
1 CONT
1 CONT Death record of Charles J. Worden, Leon, Iowa Courthouse -- "Worden, Charley Jared, d. 17 Jan 1913 Franklin Twp, Decatur Co. IA AE 61y 11m 15d; White Male; b. 2 Feb 1851 New York; Married; occ: Farmer; Burial 19 Jan 1913, Johnson 
1 CONC Cemetery, Decatur Co. IA Funeral Dir: H.O. Tuttle, Van Wert; Certifier: O.P. Jamison 22 Jan 1913; Cause of death: Lobar Pneumonia; Father: Jared N. Worden b. NY; Mother: Maria T. Denton; Informant: Mrs. Chas Worden, Weldon IA." 
1 CONT
1 CONT January 16, 1913, Weldon, Iowa Newspaper: -- "Mrs. Al Markham went to Crown last night, Tuesday, being called to the home of her parents near that place by the illness of her father, C. J. Worden." 
1 CONT
1 CONT January 23, 1913, Weldon, Iowa Newspaper, Crown Column: -- "Several from Crown attended the Charley Worden Funeral." 
1 CONT
1 CONT January 23, 1913, Weldon, Iowa Newspaper, Fairview Column: -- "Mrs. Goff of near Humeston, Mrs. Clark of Davis City, and Mrs. Markham of Leon, were called here by the illness of their father." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: 
1 CONT 1. Obituary of Charles J. Worden. 
1 CONT 2. 1913 Weldon, Iowa Newspapers. 
1 CONT 3. Descendants of Charles J. Worden. 
1 CONT 4. 1900 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census. 
1 CONT 5. 1910 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census. 
1 CONT 6. "Excerpts of Worden History", by Almeda Worden Langsdorf, 1969. 
1 CONT 7. Death Record of Charles J. Worden, County Courthouse, Leon, Iowa. 
1 CONT
1 CONT More about some of Charles' children: 
1 CONT 1. Hattie Belle Worden: 
1 CONT January 16, 1913, Weldon, Iowa Newspaper: -- "Mrs. Al Markham went to Crown last night, Tuesday, being called to the home of her parents near that place by the illness of her father, C. J. Worden." 
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. William Arthur Worden: Arthur signed a birth certificate in 1914. William "Art" Worden was born on August 28, 1881 in Lincoln County, Kansas. He married Faye Marie McBroom (1892-1976). She spent her life in Leroy, Iowa. Art die
1 CONC d on December 2, 1956 in Weldon, Iowa. They had 2 children, Helen Inez Worden (b1912) and Dorothy May Worden (b1914). Art and Faye divorced. By 1927, Faye had remarried Karl Otto Gelhaar and had 4 more children- Dean, Max, Carl, a
1 CONC nd Pauline Gelhaar. Art Worden married Lucy Caves, they had a daughter named Georgia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. Jared Newman Worden: Known as Numan (or Newman) Worden. Jared lived at home until he married, at the age of thirty. January 09, 1913, Decatur County Newspaper: -- "Worden-Stephens. On New Years day at the home of the bride in B
1 CONC loomington Township occurred the marriage of Newman Worden of Weldon and Miss Mildred Threece Stephens of Lamonia. Reverend J. S. Coffin, pastor of the Leon Christian Church officiated. The ceremony was performed promptly at noon 
1 CONC in the presence of a few neighbors and relatives and after the hearty congratulations were over, the guests were invited to the dining room where they partook of a bounteous dinner, prepared by Mrs Stephens. The groom is the son o
1 CONC f Mr. and Mrs. C. J. Worden who were early settlers of Decatur County and among the best citizens. He is an industrious young man of good habits and owns a farm in Franklin Township where he and his bride will make their home. The
1 CONC  bride is the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Stephens, who for many years have lived in the vicinity of Lamoni and are well and favorably known. The large number of friends and acquaintances wish these 2 young people all the prosp
1 CONC erity and happiness that is possible for them to have in their new relationship." 
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. Charles Elbert Worden: Charles Elbert Worden was born on July 09, 1888 in Decatur County, Iowa and died on August 14, 1936 in the same county. He never married. He was an Iowa Private 1C1 352nd Infantry Division SS Div in World
1 CONC  War I. Elbert shot himself in the finger to avoid the service, but he got called anyway! Except for his war service, Elbert lived all of his life at his parent's home place near Weldon, Iowa. He took care of his mother during her
1 CONC  last extended illness. She lingered on her sick bed for at least 2 or 3 years. She probably had uterine cancer. Elbert survived his mother by only months. He is buried in Johnson Cemetery, Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa,
1 CONC  Row 2, right next to his father and mother. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. Lottie Geneva Worden: Lottie and her husband Loyal moved to Washington State. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 6. Riley Harvey Worden: Individual: Worden, Harvey, Birth date: Sep 23, 1895, Death date: Jan 1985, Last residence: IA 50144, State of issue: IA. Harvey and Belva farmed in their early married life. Harvey spent his last years at 
1 CONC his home in Leon, Iowa. Riley Harvey Worden was still living at home with his mother and brother Charles Elbert Worden in the 1920 Census. Sources: A. Broderbund Family Archive #110, Vol. 2, Ed. 4, Social Security Death Index, B. 
1 CONC 1910 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census, C. 1920 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census, D. Riley Harvey Worden. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
0 @N6000000000953524300@ NOTE
1 CONT Edward came with his parents to Deatur county, Iowa form New York in 1856.  He was a member of the Christian Church, 100C.  Edward was a farmer.  Source:  "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahern, page 266
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Counties, Iowa, (Lewis Publishing Company - 1887), 99. 669-70:
1 CONT
1 CONT “E.T.Worden is a native of Dutchess County, New York, born in November, 1846, a son of Jared N. and Maria T. (Denton) Worden.  He was reared a farmer, receiving his education in the public schools.  When he was eleven years of age
1 CONC , in 1857, his parents moved to Decatur County, Iowa, and here he was reared to manhood.  He has always given his attention to agricultural pursuits, and now owns a fine farm of 120 acred, his building improvements as being commod
1 CONC ious and convenient.  He has entirely lost the sight of one eye, the result of a stroke from a corn-stalk.  Mr. Worden is in politics a Republican.  His wife is amember of the Christian church.  He was married in 1874, to Miss Kit
1 CONC tie Starr, daughter of Joseph and P.F.(Chapman) Starr.  They have four children - Myrtle Irene, Lewis E., Joseph E. and Fred.”
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes for EDWARD TURNER WORDEN:
1 CONT Edward came with his parents to Decatur County, Iowa from New York in 1856. He was a member of the Christian Church, IOOC. Edward was a farmer. According to the 1900 Eden Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census, Edward's name was al
1 CONC so listed as Edward Franklin Worden, he may have had both Franklin and Turner as middle names. The town where Edward lived most of his life, Weldon, was in both Clarke and Decatur Counties, Iowa, but he is mostly in Decatur County
1 CONC  records. In the 1920 Census, Edward and Kate had two grandchildren who lived with them: Ida, b. 1911 and Melvin, b. 1913, as well as his daughter Myrtle, age 42. Edward is buried in Van Wert Cemetery, Row 8, Van Wert, Decatur Cou
1 CONC nty, Iowa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT 1. The book, "Down One Hundred Years", by Dale Ahern, page 266, Leon, Iowa Public Library.
1 CONT 2. WPA Decatur County Burial Records, Decatur County Public Library, Leon, Iowa.
1 CONT 3. 1880 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census.
1 CONT 4. 1900 Eden Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census.
1 CONT 5. 1910 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census.
1 CONT 6. 1920 Franklin Township, Decatur County, Iowa Census.
1 CONT 7. LDS File submission.
0 @N6000000000953524344@ NOTE
1 CONT Phebe is buried in Row 8, stone 2, Metier Cemetery, Garden Grove, Decatur, Iowa.
0 @N6000000000953524354@ NOTE
1 CONT Notes for AMANDA MELISSA WORDEN:
1 CONT Some of the Manchesters of Decatur County, Iowa are descended from Amanda and her husband. Amanda was most likely named after her mother's sister, Amanda Denton. Source for husband's name: 1913 Obituary of Charles Jared Worden.
0 @N6000000000953525179@ NOTE
1 CONT Born Cornelius Jared WORDEN.  He came to Decatur County, IA in 1856 with his family.  He was a member of the Christian Church, 100F, he was a farmer, Source:"Down one Hundred Years", by Date Ahern, pg. 266.
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Biographical and Historical Record of Ringgold and Decatur Counties, Iowa, (Lewis Publishing Company - 1887) pp. 504-05:
1 CONT
1 CONT “Cornelius Denton Worden is native of Dutchess County, New York, born May 1, 1855, a son of Jared N. and Maria T. (Denton) Worden, and was but one year of age when his parents moved to Decatur County, and here he was reared and ed
1 CONC ucated.  He has always given his attention and agricultural pursuits, and now owns a good farm of eighty acres, where he resides, on section 16, Franklin Township.  He is a member of one of the pioneer families of Decatur County, 
1 CONC and an energetic and industrious citizen.  He was married in 1877 to Miss Frances Eleanor Rogers, daughter of William E. and Adelia (Starr) Rogers, of Washington County, Iowa.  Mr. and Mrs. Worden have one daughter - Bessie.  They
1 CONC  are members of Christian church.  Mr. Worden is a member of the Odd Fellows order.  In politics he affiliates with the Republican party.
0 @N6000000000140952790@ NOTE
1 CONT She was first married to Horace Button of Jonesville, Michigan, by whom she became the mother of one son, Charles E., who was graduated form the Philadelphia Medical Institute, and is now engaged in the drug business in Chicago. S
1 CONC he was divorced from Mr. Button in 1871, and next married Adna B. Smith of Bronson in 1872. He died 3 Dec. 1887.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. Sager has been a very successful teacher of music, which profession she followed for a number of years in La Cygne, Linn, Kansas. She owns property in this county.
